WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.211
Christina Couch: Okay, it looks like we're,
we're ready to go here. Panelists, are you

00:00:14.211 --> 00:00:16.029
all ready to do this?

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:18.180
Tanya Ballard Brown: Ready.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.190
Christina Couch: Hey, great! Okay, wonderful.
Welcome to all of the attendees who are here.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:29.380
There are quite a few of you. We are thrilled
to have you. Welcome to the MIT Communications

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.380
Forum.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.100
Christina Couch: So this is our first virtual
event. We're really thrilled to be using this

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.130
format to reach a broader audience, so sincerely,
thank you to every single person who's here

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:40.370
for making that happen.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.140
Christina Couch: We have what is bound to
be a really fascinating panel tonight. We

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:51.680
were so excited that all of our speakers and
moderator were available. So I will give everybody

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.130
a little bit of info about how this event
will work. We're going to have roughly 45

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.199
minutes of moderated Q and A followed by

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:08.020
Christina Couch: Sorry, followed by about
45 minutes of audience Q and A. I'll be reading

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.460
that audience Q and A out loud to our speakers.
So if you have a question, do not put it in

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.590
the chat. Drop it in the Q and A box below.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:20.310
Christina Couch: There are a lot more attendees
than there are speakers. So we will try to

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.520
get to as many questions as is humanly possible.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.409
Christina Couch: This event is being recorded.
We plan to add some closed captioning and

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:36.460
we're going to post it on our MIT forum, MIT
Communications Forum website at Commforum.mit.edu.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:41.390
Christina Couch: If you would like to see
more events just like this one, please sign

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:49.600
up for our mailing list. That is also at Commforum.mit.edu.
I will put that link in the chat as soon as

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.600
this intro is over.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.100
Christina Couch: I promise you we will not
spam you. We only send emails related to our

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:00.229
events each year. We only have about six and
so you won't be receiving too much email from

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:01.229
us.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:05.079
Christina Couch: One last thing, we would
not be able to make events like this happen

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:13.090
without generous support from sponsors. A
very special thank you to Radius at MIT, they've

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.200
supported many of our events.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.200
Christina Couch: They've been a phenomenal
partner for making events like this happen,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.190
and a special thanks to the MIT Governance
Lab. We're working with them for the first

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:26.970
time, and we're thrilled for their support.
So without further ado, we have a spectacular

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:27.970
lineup.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.670
Christina Couch: I'm excited to introduce
our speakers and our moderator.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:35.310
Christina Couch: First up, we have Dr. Shardé
M. Davis. She is an assistant professor in

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.920
the Department of Communication at the University
of Connecticut, and she's the creator of #BlackInTheIvory.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.000
Christina Couch: Dr Davis's research examines
the way Black women leverage communication

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.330
in the sister circle to invoke collective
identity,

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.120
Christina Couch: erect and fortify the boundaries
around their home place, and backfill the

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.470
necessary resources to return to a white / male
dominated spaces in society.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:57.690
Christina Couch: Dr. Davis also serves as
the Immediate Past Chair of the African American

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.180
Communication and Culture Division of the
National Communications Association.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:07.769
Christina Couch: Welcome, Dr. Davis. Dr. James
Mickens is the Gordon Mackay Professor of

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:11.989
Computer Science at Harvard University. His
research focuses on the performance, security,

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:14.590
and robustness of large scale distributed
web services.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.720
Christina Couch: Prior to his work at Harvard,
Dr Mickens spent six years at Microsoft Research,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.560
where he worked in the Distributed Systems
Group. He is currently on the board of directors

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.500
at the Berkman-Klein Center for Internet and
Society at Harvard. Welcome, Dr. Mickens.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:33.480
Christina Couch: Dr. Mareena Robinson Snowden
is one of MIT's own. She is a senior engineer

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.750
at the National Security.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:39.290
Christina Couch: Analysis Department at the
Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:44.019
Prior to her current role, Dr. Snowden completed
fellowships at the National Nuclear Security

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:45.019
Administration

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:48.770
Christina Couch: and the Carnegie Endowment
for International Peace Nuclear Policy Program.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:53.450
She was the first Black woman to earn a Ph.D.
in nuclear engineering from MIT. Welcome,

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:54.739
Dr. Snowden.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:58.810
Christina Couch: And our moderator tonight
is actually somebody I was lucky enough to

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:03.549
meet at a communi, excuse me, Communications
Forum event a few years ago.

00:04:03.549 --> 00:04:07.470
Christina Couch: We were thrilled that she
agreed to come back.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.409
Christina Couch: Her name is Tanya Ballard
Brown. She is an editor at National Public

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:15.500
Radio where she's worked on stories about
families of shooting victims in New Orleans,

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.109
Christina Couch: the aftermath of the Pulse
nightclub shooting, and sexual assault against

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:23.730
people with disabilities. She was a 2019 Nieman
Foundation for Journalism Fellow where she

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.130
studied the intersection of humor, satire,
and journalism.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:32.120
Projects she's edited at NPR and The Washington
Post have been awarded Peabody, Murrow, and

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.120
Gracie

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.090
awards, among others. Thank you so much to
all of our speakers. I'm going to pass it

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.750
off to Tanya right now.
Tanya Ballard Brown: Thanks everybody for

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.490
taking time out to come and join us for this
conversation

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.530
Tanya Ballard Brown: this evening. So earlier
this year as people were protesting in the

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.800
streets and demanding an

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.780
Tanya Ballard Brown: end to racial injustice
and calling for solutions to ending systemic

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.160
racism, that energy spread

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:09.020
beyond just policing and beyond the Black
Lives Matter slate of policy proposals

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.494
and into other areas, such as my industry,
media, but also into academia. And over the

00:05:13.494 --> 00:05:14.494
summer

00:05:14.494 --> 00:05:22.350
that hashtag Black in the Ivory started trending
and it gained traction as Black scholars like

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.500
all these

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:28.960
wonderful, really accomplished people that
we have here today, shared their experiences

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:29.960
confronting

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:36.732
and contending with discrimination and alienation
and other issues in colleges and universities.

00:05:36.732 --> 00:05:43.090
Tanya Ballard Brown: And so today we want
to talk about some of what was shared in the

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:44.090
tweet thread and what

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:49.690
continues to be shared, as well as best practices,
lessons learned, and how to navigate some

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:50.690
of these

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:55.910
situations that others have been struggling
with over the years. And so I want to throw

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:56.910
this first

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:02.110
to Shardé -- I'm going to call people by
their first names. I'm Tanya, too. I'm Tanya

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.110
-- who was the

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:08.650
main engine behind the hashtag. And just to
give us, just talk to us a little bit about

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:12.860
why
you did this, and what were some of the stories

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:18.150
that were shared. What trends or

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:25.240
recurring themes did you see shared when you
started this conversation and got this conversation

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.240
going.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:32.520
Shardé Davis: Absolutely. Hi, everyone. Good
evening. I'm really humbled to be in this

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.520
space

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.000
Shardé Davis: with so many brilliant minds
and to able to talk about this really pertinent

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.000
topic.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.940
Shardé Davis: One that we know has always
been around, anti-Black racism in the academy.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:44.060
We also

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:47.419
Shardé Davis: know anti-Black racism is built
into the bedrock of the United States. So

00:06:47.419 --> 00:06:49.509
naturally, it is going to

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:54.340
manifest itself in various social institutions.
And I think that's, not I think, but that

00:06:54.340 --> 00:07:01.070
really is the kind of starting point to how.
I started to think about this because I was

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.070
just reeling

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:11.410
in over the summer, quarantining because of
Covid, And, you know, kind of sitting in what

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:19.520
felt like isolation in a rental apartment
in California and out protesting, just feeling

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:20.520
like

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.970
Shardé Davis: I was trying to do something,
right? I was trying to somehow make sense

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:30.840
of who I am as a Black woman
and how my, this kind of capital C conversation

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.770
that was happening this summer, this racial

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:39.069
reckoning, how that showed up in my own life.
And because I thankfully, knock on wood, had

00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:40.069
not had

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:44.530
Shardé Davis: personal run-ins with the police
when it comes to mass incarceration, when

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:45.530
it comes to police

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:50.360
brutality, it doesn't mean that anti-Black
racism doesn't manifest in my life. And so

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:51.360
I was

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.550
just sitting there thinking about, what does
this mean to me, right? And how does this

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:56.550
show up within my

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:01.290
own immediate sphere of influence, and immediately
just started thinking about the academy and

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:05.870
my own
experiences, and just an interesting play

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:10.200
on words, right? Seemingly so simple, right?
Being Black in the Ivory for me felt like

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:14.770
a speck in a sea of white, which, growing
up in San Diego, going

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:21.629
to predominantly white institutions all of
my life in terms of secondary education, that's

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:22.629
just the

00:08:22.629 --> 00:08:27.740
recurring theme and I just remember that Sunday
when it was trending. And I think it was in

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:28.740
the

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:33.930
Shardé Davis: top 20 hashtags across the
United States that Sunday. I mean, I was just

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.349
floored that, you know, for

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:42.169
me, it just started, I just was using that
as a way to process what was happening around

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:48.200
me.
Shardé Davis: And it's crazy how it, it's

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.290
crazy but it's not how it resonated with so
many other Blackademics, right?

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:58.070
Shardé Davis: And in reading the tweets and,
I mean talk about all your plans being cancelled

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.070
that day,

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:02.130
because I was just reading. I just couldn't
stop. I wanted to make sure I was reading

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.130
every

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:08.380
single tweet and in terms of the stories that
were surfacing, I don't know. I think about

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:09.380
this phrase,

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:14.440
I've used this recently, the death by 1,000
paper cuts. So absolutely, that does not mean

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.440
that

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:22.420
many Blackademics around the globe have not
experienced overt racism. Some women had talked

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:27.140
about sexual assault, right, so thinking about
intersectionality and how we cannot untether

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:33.020
our racial identity from our gender identity
and other identities as well, sexuality and

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:34.020
age.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:41.910
Shardé Davis: But it was the micro aggressions,
the unwanted touches to someone's hair for

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.209
specifically

00:09:43.209 --> 00:09:48.130
those who were donning their natural kind
of kinky curls and other natural hairstyles,

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:54.160
Shardé Davis: being questioned about if they're
the janitor. You know what I mean, when

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:59.510
they're in a lab after hours, being asked
for identification when they're trying to

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:00.510
go into the

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:06.700
lecture hall to teach by campus police right.
So you know these incidences where again,

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:07.700
you know,

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:13.900
racial micro-aggressions is the psychological
warfare, where you're questioning is it me?

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:17.060
Shardé Davis: This seems racially motivated,
but I can't, I can't tell. I can't quite put

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:18.060
my finger on it. So it was in,

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.840
I'll just say this and just to kind of wrap
up my answer to your question, but what I

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:23.840
found

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:29.410
to be the most interesting, one of the most
interesting pieces in reading the tweets was

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:35.700
how sometimes people were sharing the exact
same story. And I mean, down to the minutia.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:41.210
Shardé Davis: And these are people who are
went to grad school at different time periods,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.210
who

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.850
Shardé Davis: are different generations in
terms of like age groups, people who, men

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.350
and women, different fields,

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:52.240
Shardé Davis: they're from programs across
the United States and saying the exact same

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:53.240
story.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:58.740
Tanya Ballard Brown: Well, thank you. I think
it is interesting that you had, you found

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.339
that there was so much,

00:11:00.339 --> 00:11:06.920
Tanya Ballard Brown: but repetition in the
stories that people were telling and that

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:07.920
segues a little

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:14.089
Tanya Ballard Brown: bit into, I want to throw
this question to James, Dr. Mickens

00:11:14.089 --> 00:11:23.470
Tanya Ballard Brown: Sociologist Tsedale M.
Melaku has described the inclusion tax, right.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:26.070
She's talked about the time spent,

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:30.370
Tanya Ballard Brown: the money spent, the
emotions spent, the brainpower spent trying

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.451
to adhere to norms in

00:11:33.451 --> 00:11:39.550
Tanya Ballard Brown: academic spaces, and
I'm curious, and you've talked about this

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.220
a little yourself in

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.170
Tanya Ballard Brown: other areas, but I'm
curious what ways you've found to push back

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:50.490
against some of that in your career.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:56.000
James Mickens: Thanks for the question and
I'm glad to be here, glad to see so many people

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.459
on the call. I think

00:11:57.459 --> 00:12:01.459
James Mickens: that that question that you
asked, I mean it's a hard one to answer because,

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:02.459
in a certain sense,

00:12:02.459 --> 00:12:06.709
James Mickens: it speaks to the fact that
there is this additional burden that scholars

00:12:06.709 --> 00:12:08.200
of color oftentimes have to

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:12.579
James Mickens: face in addition to the other
things that scholars already do that makes

00:12:12.579 --> 00:12:14.089
scholarship hard, you know,

00:12:14.089 --> 00:12:18.480
publish or perish, being on committees, you
know, all this type of stuff. And so it's

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:19.480
difficult. You know,

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:24.450
I think that one reason why it's difficult
is because it can be hard to tell someone,

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:29.709
look I know that you're busy, but I'm actually
probably a little bit busier than you

00:12:29.709 --> 00:12:32.000
because of the various committees, for example,
that I'm asked to

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:36.649
be on that you might not be asked to be on,
not because you're a bad person. Just because,

00:12:36.649 --> 00:12:39.529
you know, people are looking for the diversity
unit,

00:12:39.529 --> 00:12:44.139
you know. And so I think that one of the things
that I try to coach young academics on,

00:12:44.139 --> 00:12:50.660
that I try to be very intentional about my
own career is being able to, to be frank and

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:51.660
to be polite.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:56.610
James Mickens: But to be open about the fact
that there are certain things that I want

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:57.610
to do

00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:01.529
because I want to increase, for example, diversity
in professoriate. There are certain things

00:13:01.529 --> 00:13:02.529
I'm

00:13:02.529 --> 00:13:05.839
asked to do that perhaps I don't necessarily
want to do, but I feel like I should do as

00:13:05.839 --> 00:13:06.839
a service.

00:13:06.839 --> 00:13:09.430
James Mickens: But then that means that there
are some things I simply won't have time to

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:10.430
do.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:15.260
And I think that it's, it's sort of like more
of an art than a science when you need to

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:16.260
convey

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:20.399
these messages, when you need to tell someone
who might be, for example, your chair or your

00:13:20.399 --> 00:13:21.399
dean or

00:13:21.399 --> 00:13:24.720
someone like this, your direct manager if
you're an industry, that I would love to do

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.959
this but, you know,

00:13:26.959 --> 00:13:31.380
ellipses, I'm doing these other things. And
so one thing that I found very helpful is

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:35.360
that first of all, when you're having one
of these conversations, you need to figure

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:36.360
out, am

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:39.080
I dealing with someone who's reasonable or
not? I mean, just to put it bluntly, is this

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:40.080
a person

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.580
who you can actually negotiate with, who you
can sort of trust to sort of be sympathetic

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:44.580
with you

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.590
James Mickens: even if they don't immediately
understand what your current burdens are?

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:48.839
Because if the answer is

00:13:48.839 --> 00:13:53.490
no, if it's not a reasonable person, that's
like a whole other sort of, you know, path

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:54.490
and decision

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:59.220
tree as engineers might say. But if it's someone
who is reasonable, then oftentimes I find

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:00.220
it very

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:03.860
effective and actually very educational for
both people to say, "Here's what my current

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:04.860
state of

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:08.899
things looks like. I'm on this many committees.
I'm, you know, mentoring this many students.

00:14:08.899 --> 00:14:09.899
You know,

00:14:09.899 --> 00:14:12.851
this is my teaching load. This is what I'm
doing with respect to research. So here's

00:14:12.851 --> 00:14:13.851
what I think

00:14:13.851 --> 00:14:18.709
my priorities are. What do you think my priorities
should be?" And, you know, if you phrase that

00:14:18.709 --> 00:14:19.709
in sort

00:14:19.709 --> 00:14:23.960
of a polite way, but in sort of an honest
way and a direct way, I think that can actually

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:29.170
sort of recalibrate how people think sometimes
about what the burden of a scholar of color

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:30.170
is.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:33.760
James Mickens: Because I think there's a large
set of people in academia who are well intentioned,

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:34.760
but ignorant.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:38.000
And just to be clear, that includes me, that
includes everyone. We're all sort of ignorant

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:42.709
about something. But I think there's a large
set of people in academia, who, they're not

00:14:42.709 --> 00:14:43.709
in the Klan.

00:14:43.709 --> 00:14:46.690
They're not, you know, quote unquote "obvious
racists." They're not burning crosses in people's

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:47.690
front yard

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:54.589
and yet, you know, they're actually without
knowing it, complicit is too strong of a word,

00:14:54.589 --> 00:14:55.589
but

00:14:55.589 --> 00:14:57.519
they don't understand that, you know, we're
all embedded in sort of this larger social

00:14:57.519 --> 00:14:58.699
structure

00:14:58.699 --> 00:15:03.079
and we all play a certain role in making sure
that certain things that have inertia either

00:15:03.079 --> 00:15:07.100
continue or don't continue. So I've actually
found that very useful to say, "This is what

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:08.100
my next year

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:11.649
looks like. So, you know, you're asking me
to do this. Do you think that, you know, how

00:15:11.649 --> 00:15:12.649
do you think this

00:15:12.649 --> 00:15:16.240
fits into my overall plan? Do you think I
should maybe drop something else?" You know,

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:17.240
sometimes they

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.240
say yes. Sometimes they'll say, yeah, I think
you should actually you know do this instead

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:21.240
of that,

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:24.820
and that's good feedback for you to hear,
perhaps as well. But I think that one of the

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:25.820
big

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:30.350
challenges I see for scholars of color is
that they try to say yes to everything,

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:34.880
James Mickens: in part because of a fear of
being sort of stereotyped as sort of the the

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:36.070
angry person of color

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:40.320
or, you know, this is someone, you know, "Well,
when I asked Steve to do this, Steve didn't

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.670
complain about this,"

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:46.920
you know. So I think there is a fine line
there, but I think that in general, in life,

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:47.920
it's

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:50.850
very helpful to be intentional about the things
that you do. You know when you want to get

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:51.850
some

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.350
professional or personal goal, think explicitly
about what steps you're going to take to get

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.350
there.

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:01.070
James Mickens: And I think this sort of advice
to be intentional is doubly important in academia,

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:05.380
in part because there's so many people who
can ask you what to do, right, and try to

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:06.380
suggest

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:09.070
that you do certain things. You know, I used
to spend some time in industry and that's

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:10.070
one

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:13.949
of the biggest differences I found between
industry and academia. In industry, I nominally

00:16:13.949 --> 00:16:15.380
have one boss,

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:19.940
you know? In academia, anyone can send me
an email. You know, students can send me an

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:21.130
email, people,

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:24.590
other professors can send me an email. So
it's really important to be intentional about

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:26.480
how you choose what you do.

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:32.160
Tanya Ballard Brown: I want to throw that
same question to Dr. Snowden. Mareena, in

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:33.769
terms of how you have

00:16:33.769 --> 00:16:39.630
Tanya Ballard Brown: learned to manage some
of those expectations, some of that additional

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:41.361
work, that laborious work

00:16:41.361 --> 00:16:48.840
Tanya Ballard Brown: that maybe someone not
of color is not having to take on

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:54.670
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Yeah, you know,
it's really, I think that James and Shardé

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:57.120
really just hit the nail on the head with

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:01.790
Mareena Robinson Snowden: so many pieces of
their answer, right. So, so, you know, James

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:02.790
was talking about

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:06.660
Mareena Robinson Snowden: this honesty and
transparency right and owning the fact that

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:08.780
your time, you know, time is the most

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:14.730
limited resource, right, and oftentimes we
struggle with saying no, right. That is what

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:15.730
James is saying,

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:21.549
you need to be able to say no. To be intentional
is to have to prioritize, right, and one of

00:17:21.549 --> 00:17:22.549
the hardest

00:17:22.549 --> 00:17:28.620
things, I think, scholars and practitioners
of color have to deal with is prioritizing

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:29.620
ourselves

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:35.220
because the environment seems so unstable
for us, right. Because you are often the only

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:36.220
one in

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:43.790
the room, so every decision that you make
feels so consequential. Even in reality, it

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:46.020
may or may not be, right?

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:50.270
We recognizes and acknowledge that there is
perception and then there's also

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:56.669
reality on both sides of the equation. Right.
Um, but I think this, this, this skill and

00:17:56.669 --> 00:17:57.669
ability, which

00:17:57.669 --> 00:18:03.730
is something that is built, right, to be able
to prioritize yourself and your career and

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.400
say no

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:11.220
is key. Right. Now it's interesting because,
for me, you know, I was steeped and raised

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:12.520
in the tradition

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:18.960
of Historically Black Colleges and Universities.
Right. And so much of that, I'm a proud graduate

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:24.170
of Florida A&amp;M University. So before I had
anything to do with MIT, I was a rattler,

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:25.710
okay and the rattler perspective.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:28.450
Tanya Ballard Brown: I'm North Carolina A&amp;T

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:34.510
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Come on now, Come
on now. Okay. But that, that HBCU perspective

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:41.580
is about, to a degree selflessness. Right.
It's about putting yourself in a position

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.030
to help your culture, right.

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:49.460
So there is a balance that has to be struck
between being so laser focused on your own

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:50.510
success

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:56.929
and because it is a testament and a data point&nbsp;for
your culture about what can happen, and then

00:18:56.929 --> 00:19:03.720
kind of what I feel as a duty to the culture
to contribute to this work, right.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:07.970
So, the committee work that James and Shardé
talked about, right. Like there, for me,

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:12.620
Mareena Robinson Snowden: it's not, I never
feel comfortable because of that sense of

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:15.150
duty saying no to everything, right.

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:18.900
Like you have to make space for that. And
I think that's, you know, I think James

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:24.880
and Shardé would agree. You know, Shardé
talked about this, this question of is it

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:25.980
me, right?

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:31.490
Like they asked, they stopping me? Like am
I tripping? Like, did I take that the wrong

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:32.490
way? When the guy,

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:38.679
I was at a holiday party in graduate school
and one of the other graduate students walked

00:19:38.679 --> 00:19:39.679
up to

00:19:39.679 --> 00:19:43.510
me and just, in a very well intentioned way
right James, walked up to me and say, you

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:44.510
know, I'm just,

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:50.210
you know, I know, I used to live near the
inner city and I'm so proud that you were

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:51.220
able to make

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:56.210
it out of the inner city. Right. And I'm sitting
at the holiday party with my little cup of

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:57.210
food

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:03.070
or to plate of food thinking do I respond
to this? Now, what do I say to that, right?

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:04.070
Because

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:09.710
there's so many assumptions built into that
right. Because I am a Black person in this

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:10.710
room, I must

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:17.130
have either been the affirmative action hire
or admit, or I've traversed, you know, crack

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:23.000
needles and all kinds of things to get here,
right. And there are people who live, you

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:24.610
know, our,

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:30.580
our community is so diverse, right. We have
so many different stories, from cultural,

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.020
right, like we have

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:36.020
Jamaicans, we have Haitians, we have people
from the African continent, we have people

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:37.040
from Canada,

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:43.350
like Black people are very diverse and broad
right and and to have to come up against

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:49.480
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the assumptions
can be challenging. For me, it was, it was

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:52.650
critical while I was at MIT and now

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:59.500
that I'm at Johns Hopkins to find community
right, because community is the antidote to

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:00.500
gaslighting.

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:03.990
Mareena Robinson Snowden: So when you have
a community of folks that you can go back

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:06.760
to and say, "So this thing happened

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:13.770
to me. Am I trippin?" They can say, "No, you're
not tripping," right. And Shardé's hashtag

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:14.770
was that

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:20.809
in the digital space, right. She created community
in the digital space so people could acknowledge

00:21:20.809 --> 00:21:27.970
and corroborate and endorse, right, your experience
and say, "No, you are not crazy. This does

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:28.970
happen

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:34.870
and this is wrong and it exists" to James's
point in this kind of systematic context that

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:35.870
matters,

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:40.890
right. So, you know, James is exactly right.
It is a hard question. But I think you know

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:41.890
community.

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:49.200
Mareena Robinson Snowden: is essential and
learning how to be transparent, not, not necessarily

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:51.800
vulnerable. Okay, but how to be

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:56.730
honest with your abilities at that moment,
and your priorities at that moment is key

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.120
to kind of staying along the path.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:03.630
Tanya Ballard Brown: You say community, are
you like, are you telling people to

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:07.510
Tanya Ballard Brown: seek out people like
yourself instructors who can sort of guide

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:09.370
them to other people or,

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:16.419
Tanya Ballard Brown: or are there resources
that should be your university should be compiling

00:22:16.419 --> 00:22:17.640
and making available?

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:23.780
Tanya Ballard Brown: Like, what kind, what,
what kinds of ways do people create these

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:24.780
communities?

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:28.050
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Yeah. So the easy
answer is all of the above. Right. Um, what

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:30.539
I was thinking about was, for me,

00:22:30.539 --> 00:22:36.159
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the most transformative
and kind of fortifying force while I was at

00:22:36.159 --> 00:22:38.370
MIT and now were my peers,

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:43.420
Mareena Robinson Snowden: right, were my homegirls
and my homeboys who I could, you know, W.E.B.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:44.669
Du Bois talks about the

00:22:44.669 --> 00:22:51.549
mask right. And you could drop the mask and
just be yourself and speak in your language

00:22:51.549 --> 00:22:52.549
and

00:22:52.549 --> 00:22:56.890
Mareena Robinson Snowden: you know, admit
things that you would never admit in the mainstream

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.000
conversation because of that fear of

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:03.490
vulnerability and that stereotype threat,
right, that, that you feel like if you admit

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:04.490
this,

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:07.980
Mareena Robinson Snowden: it's a damnation
on your culture. So I think it's peer community,

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:10.230
but you bring up a very key point.

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:13.720
Mareena Robinson Snowden: The best at, one
of the best pieces of advice that I got when

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:15.500
I was coming up at MIT was,

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:22.530
you have to get people to buy into your success.
So there are people in power in these institutions,

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:27.320
right, whether they're in the provost's office,
whether they're in the dean's office, right,

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:32.990
and they need to know that you exist and you
ain't playing. Right, like you serious business.

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:33.990
You good

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:40.000
at your job, so when the inevitable foolishness
starts because it always gonna be some foolishness,

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:45.929
Mareena Robinson Snowden: you already have
the reputation that no, no, no it's not her

00:23:45.929 --> 00:23:48.000
or it's not him. Something else is

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:53.679
up here because I know this person is a serious
scholar and the quality of their work and

00:23:53.679 --> 00:23:54.679
the type

00:23:54.679 --> 00:23:58.690
of their character, right, so they can start
to question their own system like something's

00:23:58.690 --> 00:24:04.170
wrong here. If she's having trouble and she's
a stellar student or scholar or professor,

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:08.100
Mareena Robinson Snowden: we must be dropping
the ball somewhere, right, so it's communities

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:09.200
at all levels, Tanya.

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:13.880
Tanya Ballard Brown: This brings me back to
Shardé because earlier this year in an interview,

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:14.880
you talked about

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:20.170
Tanya Ballard Brown: how white academics have
not given weight or attention to systemic

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:23.490
racism. And so my question

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:28.490
Tanya Ballard Brown: sort of also it builds
on what Mareena was just saying is with, you

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:29.600
know, white faculty and

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:36.671
staff making up like 70% of campuses nationwide,
what do you see is their role in helping to

00:24:36.671 --> 00:24:44.960
Tanya Ballard Brown: dismantle systemic racism?
Do you, that's a hard question. This is for

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:46.140
Shardé though.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:52.539
Shardé Davis: Right. [laughs] Yeah
Tanya Ballard Brown: What would you have them

00:24:52.539 --> 00:24:53.539
do?

00:24:53.539 --> 00:25:01.559
Shardé Davis: They have a lot to do. They
have a lot to do. Can I have to just say Mareena's

00:25:01.559 --> 00:25:02.559
preaching. Okay. She

00:25:02.559 --> 00:25:05.799
Shardé Davis: took this from like a webinar,
a forum to some kitchen table talk. I'm here

00:25:05.799 --> 00:25:06.799
for everything

00:25:06.799 --> 00:25:11.770
Shardé Davis: that she that, she said and
if I can lean into my research in terms of

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:12.770
the communities

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:19.130
Shardé Davis: and peers and the necessity
of having, for Black women, for example, a

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:20.130
sister circle, right.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:24.980
Shardé Davis: Thinking about the identities
that you hold right and where you sit and

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:26.350
your vantage point and finding

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:31.800
other individuals who are situated similarly
to you. And that can also mean other, for

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:32.800
example, other

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:36.120
Blackademics and creating a community. They
don't even necessarily have to be Blackademics

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:41.429
at your institution but even other Blackacademics
nationwide, right, because they understand

00:25:41.429 --> 00:25:42.429
what it

00:25:42.429 --> 00:25:46.390
means to be one of the only right in in that
space, but also thinking about, sometimes

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:47.390
you just need

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:52.430
to lean on your sister girls. Right and even
though they might not necessarily be academics

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:58.770
or affiliated with the university, there is
a shared understanding about our

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:05.950
double jeopardy, right, being both Black and
women. And ways in which they might be confronting

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:10.299
gendered racism within their own sphere of
influence, within their own job that might

00:26:10.299 --> 00:26:11.510
overlap or read

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:17.940
similarly to what we experience so, yeah,
I just, I just had to just kind of cosign

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:18.940
with what she was

00:26:18.940 --> 00:26:25.380
what she was preaching on. Um, white folks,
white academics, what, what is it that they

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:26.380
can

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:32.570
do? There's a lot that they can do. One, I've
been thinking about this a lot and I really

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:33.570
think

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:38.580
some of them need to have an introspective
moment about

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:44.440
Shardé Davis: about their own, the seeds
that have been planted by their parents, by

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:45.440
other socializing

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:49.011
Shardé Davis: agents, by their grandparents,
right. I mean, many of us are only one to

00:26:49.011 --> 00:26:50.140
two generations away

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:59.940
Shardé Davis: from, you know, white communities,
other communities of color who have been spewing

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:04.220
anti-Black rhetoric and and have been very
unabashed, in doing so, right. So,

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:08.480
Shardé Davis: it’s, to think that, you
know, someone's aunt, someone's grandparents

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:10.390
or great grandparents who were

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:15.470
a part of those communities, who are part
of perpetuating that rhetoric, to think that

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:21.350
they have no influence on you, right is, that’s
foolish. So I think white individuals need

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:22.350
to have an

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:26.120
introspective moment about what has been planted
inside of them, what beliefs, albeit some

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:27.120
maybe

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:32.480
deep seated right that are, that are, that
are situated, perhaps even in their subconscious,

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:36.429
and really interrogating that. Many people
are, you know, we talked about therapy, this

00:27:36.429 --> 00:27:37.730
is also another

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:42.211
Shardé Davis: topic that we should be, white
folks especially should be talking about in

00:27:42.211 --> 00:27:43.211
therapy right.

00:27:43.211 --> 00:27:47.370
Shardé Davis: How they might be complicit
or how they might be, again, perpetuating

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.559
this in just their everyday walks of life.

00:27:49.559 --> 00:27:54.100
Shardé Davis: I also think white folks need
to think about not only what their work life

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:55.100
looks

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:58.590
like, but oftentimes what you do at work is
a reflection of your own personal life.

00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:04.020
How many, look at your own friendship network.
Do you only hang around white folks?

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:08.510
Shardé Davis: Think about the books that
you read. Is it mainly white authors? Think

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:09.510
about

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:13.340
the restaurants that you patron. Are they
mainly owned by white people? Is it only serving

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:14.340
kind of

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:20.480
standard American food? Think about the, the,
the, your leisure activities, your gym

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:26.490
Shardé Davis: or other classes that you take,
fitness classes that you take, thinking about

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:27.490
different kinds of

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:32.770
seminars or workshops in your community that
you attend. Who else is in the room? Who's

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:33.770
teaching it?

00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:38.541
What are they talking about? That's a reflection
of you and your beliefs, your desires, your

00:28:38.541 --> 00:28:39.610
ideas right.

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:43.520
Again, sometimes it's subconscious. We're
subconsciously making these decisions, but

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:44.520
what

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:50.060
we believe is going to guide how we behave.
So I think that, you know, there needs to

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:51.060
be, again,

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:54.580
an introspective moment about where someone
is situated personally in their own personal

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:55.580
life.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:59.550
Shardé Davis: Um, and I don't want to say
before, but that definitely needs to be working

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:00.550
in tandem with

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:05.000
the work that they're doing at the university.
I also think that white academics absolutely

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:10.940
Shardé Davis: need to, well I don't want
to say stop, but to stop offloading this work

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.510
on to blackademics

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:17.960
and even on to other people of color. We all
have a role. That could mean making a concerted

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:18.960
effort to

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:24.890
Shardé Davis: to mentor and advise other
Black graduate students. That is not just

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:25.890
the responsibility

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:30.440
of Black faculty to advise and / or mentor
Black graduate students and Black undergraduate

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:31.440
students,

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:35.310
absolutely not. They have a role in that as
well. That could mean sacrificing their privilege,

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:36.310
de-centering their privilege

00:29:36.310 --> 00:29:40.970
and sacrificing, I shouldn't say sacrificing,
but sacrificing their,

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:46.740
Shardé Davis: their I guess comfort. Right.
But I mean, white folks benefit from privilege

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:47.919
and that then allows them

00:29:47.919 --> 00:29:52.570
to move up and matriculate the ladder, to
maneuver the university in a way that's much

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:53.570
easier.

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:57.220
Shardé Davis: than their Black counterparts.
So there's a way in which they need to sacrifice

00:29:57.220 --> 00:29:58.419
that. There's a way in which

00:29:58.419 --> 00:30:02.990
they need to sacrifice that comfort to create
room so that we can start, start to maneuver,

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:03.990
so that some of

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:09.600
our needs and desires can now be centered.
Um, there's a way in which they need to, if

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:10.600
they find

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:17.070
themselves in, I was talking about this with
some faculty of color who I work with

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:22.820
at UConn and how there are some open positions
for admin in the administrative

00:30:22.820 --> 00:30:28.820
kind of realm or tier of our university. If
white folks find themselves getting tapped

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:29.820
on the

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:34.610
shoulder, perhaps they need to think about
some of their Black peers or their Black colleagues

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:40.659
who are also more than qualified even to be
in that position, and perhaps not go and to

00:30:40.659 --> 00:30:41.659
take

00:30:41.659 --> 00:30:44.300
that and to go and suggest someone else's
name, put someone else's name in the ring,

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:45.300
right.

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:49.710
Shardé Davis: Um, there are even just small
micro behaviors that folks can do. I learned

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:50.710
this theory

00:30:50.710 --> 00:30:56.039
called shine theory, which is when in meetings,
for example, if you hear me say an idea and

00:30:56.039 --> 00:31:01.659
Shardé Davis: to go and to repeat that idea
because 9 times out of 10, no one's going

00:31:01.659 --> 00:31:02.659
to either hear it

00:31:02.659 --> 00:31:05.149
Shardé Davis: or they're not going to give
me the acknowledgement. They're going to maybe

00:31:05.149 --> 00:31:06.730
like the idea, but not

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:11.641
going to attach it to myself and they're going
to go and then take the, basically co-op the

00:31:11.641 --> 00:31:12.641
idea, right.

00:31:12.641 --> 00:31:16.420
So with shine theory, it’s saying that people
in positions of power,

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:21.990
to repeat it and to cite it so that not only
the idea gets goes forth, but also the individual

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:22.990
who

00:31:22.990 --> 00:31:29.769
owns it. Right. They then are tethered to
it. So even something as simple as

00:31:29.769 --> 00:31:35.990
making sure that we're not getting erased
and that we are getting our proper dues. Gosh,

00:31:35.990 --> 00:31:40.809
I mean, there's just, I could go on and on.
I'm sure we all could kind of collate all

00:31:40.809 --> 00:31:45.210
these various things that they could do in
their personal life and their professional

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:48.440
life. Right. And I think you got to do the
research, say it again.

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:52.260
Tanya Ballard Brown: I want to give James
and Mareena a chance to to share some ideas

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:53.690
about what they, what changes

00:31:53.690 --> 00:32:00.460
Tanya Ballard Brown: they think that white
faculty members and other academics might

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:02.549
be able to do to help

00:32:02.549 --> 00:32:06.059
Tanya Ballard Brown: dismantle some of the
systemic racism.

00:32:06.059 --> 00:32:10.400
James Mickens: Well, I think, as always, you
know, your track record of asking difficult

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:11.400
questions continues.

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:15.390
James Mickens: I appreciate that you're keeping
us on our feet. What I think is sort of underlying

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:21.230
James Mickens: That's right. We wanted the
best, we got the best. I think that one of

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:22.230
the things which is sort

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:24.650
James Mickens: of underlying your question,
which I think is interesting, and I'll just

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:25.980
put it out there bluntly.

00:32:25.980 --> 00:32:31.510
I think one of the big challenges in academia
is what do with the case of the white

00:32:31.510 --> 00:32:37.040
progressive who voted for Obama? Right, there's
like a great scene in Get Out that sort of

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:38.040
addresses

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:41.840
this very thing. You know, like, you'll find
these people in academia, who, you know, they

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:42.840
want

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:46.549
to believe they vote the right way. You know,
like, oh yeah, I voted for Obama. I totally

00:32:46.549 --> 00:32:47.549
believe in

00:32:47.549 --> 00:32:51.370
policies X, Y, and Z. But so to get into something
that Shardé was saying, it's like, but are

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:52.370
you

00:32:52.370 --> 00:32:57.020
living a life, you know, sort of academically,
in sort of personally, that reflect how you

00:32:57.020 --> 00:32:58.020
think

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:02.440
that you're voting preferences is sort of,
you know, making claims on on on the world.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:06.269
So, for example, like, and this is something,
as I mentioned, my some of the other panelists.

00:33:06.269 --> 00:33:11.721
James Mickens: Not just women and people of
color can be on diversity committees. It's

00:33:11.721 --> 00:33:13.220
possible that, you know,

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:15.950
white men can be on those committees. When
you're looking at, you know, how are we going

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:16.950
to manage

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:23.740
James Mickens: You know, a daycare for for
professors who have children, it doesn't just

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:24.740
have to be women

00:33:24.740 --> 00:33:27.760
on that committee. When we're looking at,
to Mareena’s point, looking at, well, how

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:28.760
can

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:30.960
we increase the diversity of our graduate
student admins,

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:34.990
it doesn't just have to be people who have
the demographic characteristics that you want

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:38.340
to improve being on that committee.
And so I think that

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:42.049
it's important, and once again, this is another
one of those difficult conversations that

00:33:42.049 --> 00:33:43.049
you,

00:33:43.049 --> 00:33:48.010
you have to sort of midwife with some grace
and with some, some charm. But I think it's

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:49.240
important

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:54.789
to ask people to basically step up to these
things to say, you know, can you act as an

00:33:54.789 --> 00:33:55.789
ally,

00:33:55.789 --> 00:34:00.480
because I think, in many cases, that's really
what's lacking in in academia. Not just people

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:04.500
who say, like, “Yes, I believe in X, Y,
and Z,” but it's like will you actually

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:06.210
perform explicit

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:12.359
acts of allyship? You know, if you see, for
example, that, you know, a lot of committee

00:34:12.359 --> 00:34:14.310
work is being

00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:19.160
pushed towards people of color, will you be
an ally and suggest maybe I'll do some of

00:34:19.160 --> 00:34:20.160
that

00:34:20.160 --> 00:34:23.460
work, or I'll suggest someone else who is
under-burdened who should maybe get some of

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:24.460
that work?

00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:27.440
James Mickens: And there are very simple,
very practical things that I think that,

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:31.510
James Mickens: you know, allies can do. So,
for example, when you're thinking about colloquium

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:32.510
speakers.

00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:36.530
James Mickens: Who should we invite to come
talk to our students as exemplars of what

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:37.849
academia should look like?

00:34:37.849 --> 00:34:41.409
James Mickens: You know, a very simple question
that you can ask in a meeting is, have we

00:34:41.409 --> 00:34:42.570
consider people of color here?

00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:46.349
Is there anyone that we're missing? Are there
any women that we're missing here? You know,

00:34:46.349 --> 00:34:47.349
there's anyone

00:34:47.349 --> 00:34:51.159
with a disability, things like this? And what's
interesting is that when you ask that question,

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:56.460
James Mickens: just asking the question, a
lot of people will say, oh, right, right,

00:34:56.460 --> 00:34:57.810
why don't we invite

00:34:57.810 --> 00:35:02.640
so and so? And so was just that sort of gentle
prodding that was necessary, but someone has

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:03.640
to

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:06.770
do it. You know, someone has to do it, and
you have to sort of set this culture of people

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:07.770
being willing

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:12.130
to ask those questions. And you know, I also
want to really sort of emphasize something

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:13.130
that Mareena

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:15.670
said, which has already been emphasized by
Shardé, but particularly if we have a lot

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:16.670
of engineers in

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:21.460
the audience. Even in engineering, it is a
social science. There are plenty of people

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:22.460
who are super

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:27.180
smart super bright who didn't build the right
network and you don't hear about those folks.

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:31.760
And so I think it's very important, you know,
to build that community and it can't just

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:32.760
be, you know,

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:36.190
people of color in the answer. And, you know,
why is that? Because there's not a lot of

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:37.190
us out there

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:41.310
right now. And so, you know, if you're just
going to hitch your wagon to just a colored

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:42.310
star, your wagon might

00:35:42.310 --> 00:35:47.240
not be going very far at a lot of universities,
you know. So I think that if you do have allies,

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:48.240
you

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:51.480
know, who are white, you should you should
allow them to help you because these people

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:52.480
do exist out

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:56.820
there, you know, but I do want to r- emphasize
that point once again that community is so

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:57.820
important.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:01.230
James Mickens: Because, you know, when you
think about, you know, who gets academic jobs,

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:02.640
academia is very small,

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:06.570
you know, and as I always tell my students
there's sort of like the long line and the

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:07.570
short line, just

00:36:07.570 --> 00:36:10.240
like get into a club. You know, you always
want to be in that short line. Right. And

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:11.329
so how do you get

00:36:11.329 --> 00:36:16.680
on that short line? You get on the short line
by knowing people and by your allies knowing

00:36:16.680 --> 00:36:17.680
people.

00:36:17.680 --> 00:36:23.180
And so you want to be very intentional regardless
of what stage of your career you’re at.

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:24.380
You could be a grad student. You could be
a professor. You can be emeritus. It doesn't

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:29.430
matter. You can be retired, right, there's
still things in your life that you want, and

00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:30.430
you want to

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:36.070
be intentional about building the community
that will allow you to achieve those goals.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:42.849
Tanya Ballard Brown: I’ll toss this to Mareena,
but what you talked about this a little bit

00:36:42.849 --> 00:36:43.849
about the

00:36:43.849 --> 00:36:49.160
Tanya Ballard Brown: culture that BIPOC students
encounter when they're coming to universities

00:36:49.160 --> 00:36:50.160
and they're

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:54.560
Tanya Ballard Brown: not necessarily ready
for the campus culture, and just sort of navigating

00:36:54.560 --> 00:37:00.600
Tanya Ballard Brown: some of these things.
What responsibility do institutions have to

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:02.119
try to help students,

00:37:02.119 --> 00:37:08.240
Tanya Ballard Brown: not just get to the school,
but get through the school and all of these

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:09.690
things that you're talking about?

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:14.500
Tanya Ballard Brown: And what are some of
the things that they might be thinking about

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:15.500
instituting

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:20.810
Tanya Ballard Brown: to help better help students
matriculating, graduate, and be successful

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:21.810
because our numbers

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:27.800
Tanya Ballard Brown: show that we some we
can get there sometimes, but we don't always

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:30.690
get finished. We don’t always cross the
finish line.

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:34.710
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Absolutely. You
know, I think, to your question, what, what

00:37:34.710 --> 00:37:36.000
proportion of the responsibility, I say,

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:39.850
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the vast majority
of the responsibility falls on the institution.

00:37:39.850 --> 00:37:40.850
Right. And the first thing

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:44.970
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the institution
needs to do is know thyself. Right, like one

00:37:44.970 --> 00:37:49.990
of the biggest errors I see consistently

00:37:49.990 --> 00:37:53.570
Mareena Robinson Snowden: even now in the
30 years of diversity and equity and inclusion

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:55.410
being mainstream right, like this is

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:58.320
Mareena Robinson Snowden: not a new conversation
we’re having, and that's why this is so

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:00.220
frustrating, right, because this work,

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:05.400
Mareena Robinson Snowden: you know, was instigated
in like really hardcore in the 90s, and it

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:07.280
was the same situation right.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:11.660
Mareena Robinson Snowden: And the problem
is that institutions, you know, it's very

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:14.160
easy. I see that there's a lot of different

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:21.850
kind of facets to DEI work, right, and, you
know, one of the trends. I see is that institutions

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:29.560
of higher education, industry, military, regardless,
government. Everyone is very comfortable with

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:30.859
the recruitment

00:38:30.859 --> 00:38:37.560
conversation, right. How do we get these shining
stars into our institution and once they

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:41.700
get through the door, it’s going to be kumbaya
cause it’s okay, right. Unfortunately, as

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:42.700
you

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:46.110
said, Tanya the data does not bear that out,
right. Students are getting here with all

00:38:46.110 --> 00:38:47.110
of the vigor

00:38:47.110 --> 00:38:52.180
and enthusiasm and promise, and for whatever
reason they're leaving with a masters when

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:53.180
they applied

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:57.270
for a Ph.D. They're leaving before the tenure
track happens or they're not getting tenure

00:38:57.270 --> 00:38:58.380
at all.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:05.950
Right. And what I find is a consistent error
is that institutions are not tracking this

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:10.270
and if they are tracking it, they're not tracking
it in an actionable way. They're not setting

00:39:10.270 --> 00:39:11.270
any

00:39:11.270 --> 00:39:17.560
strategies around these numbers, right. I
think the most guarded data in higher ed institutions

00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:21.250
is your demographic data and it's guarded
because people are embarrassed, because the

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:22.780
numbers are not

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:27.450
moving in the right direction and they don't
want to be shamed. Right. But we have to move

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:28.450
out of this

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:34.340
frame of embarrassment and shame and just
acknowledge as a collective community under

00:39:34.340 --> 00:39:39.250
the context of the legacy of slavery and Jim
Crow and the transatlantic slave trade, right,

00:39:39.250 --> 00:39:44.940
like nobody is doing this right. So you're
not uniquely failing in this. We need to get

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:45.940
beyond

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:50.359
the shame, so that we can share, right, and
we can we can strategize together about how

00:39:50.359 --> 00:39:51.560
to correct these

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:57.859
trends specifically with respect to cultural
and ethnic diversity, right. Because another

00:39:57.859 --> 00:39:58.859
thing

00:39:58.859 --> 00:40:02.720
that institutions of higher education are
very comfortable with is defining diversity

00:40:02.720 --> 00:40:03.720
in

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:10.700
terms of gender. Right. We are very quick
to talk about how we have achieved at our

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:11.700
beloved MIT,

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:16.460
achieved gender parity in the undergraduate
ranks right. We are near 50/50. The proportion

00:40:16.460 --> 00:40:17.460
of

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:21.700
women in STEM is rising, year after year.
But as those same people, how many black folks

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:22.740
they have

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:28.190
in their programs? And what the 10-year trends
look like? They're stagnant, if not reducing

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:33.960
ight, so we have to hold ourselves accountable
to the way that we're defining these terms,

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:34.960
right.

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:39.260
You're defining them in a way that suits you,
in a way that you're proud to talk about their

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:40.260
data,

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:44.950
but if you're not willing to know thyself
and look at yourself, then where are we? We

00:40:44.950 --> 00:40:45.950
lost before

00:40:45.950 --> 00:40:52.010
we even started. You know, um, I think I,
you know, I just, James, you are the MVP talking

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:53.010
about this

00:40:53.010 --> 00:40:58.089
white progressive, my brother. Okay. Because
I read something recently that said, it really

00:40:58.089 --> 00:40:59.089
ties in

00:40:59.089 --> 00:41:02.480
so well with, it was a Harvard Business Review
article or something and they were talking

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:04.190
about

00:41:04.190 --> 00:41:09.831
kind of the other side of, the the other side
of the negative diversity corner where you

00:41:09.831 --> 00:41:16.230
have folks that say, “Listen, we have Asian-Americans.
You know, we have a transgender person,

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:22.520
a lesbian person, a gay person. We have a,
you know, a differently abled person.

00:41:22.520 --> 00:41:27.359
Like aren't really diverse enough? Like we
did it. We're here.” But, but the question

00:41:27.359 --> 00:41:28.470
is, where, where are the,

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:33.150
where's the Black person? Like you ain't done
until you have Black people. And the reason

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:34.150
why

00:41:34.150 --> 00:41:38.609
you're not done until you have Black people
there is because you have to frame it in terms

00:41:38.609 --> 00:41:39.609
of impact

00:41:39.609 --> 00:41:44.030
Mareena Robinson Snowden: right. Diversity
and inclusion work is about who is the most

00:41:44.030 --> 00:41:47.099
marginalized? Who is impacted? It's not

00:41:47.099 --> 00:41:50.910
about your intention. I understand what you
meant to say, right, but that's not what's

00:41:50.910 --> 00:41:51.910
important here.

00:41:51.910 --> 00:41:58.060
It's about how your actions land on communities.
Right. And the most effective, still to this

00:41:58.060 --> 00:41:59.060
day,

00:41:59.060 --> 00:42:03.849
Covid is bearing it out, day after day, right,
if the Black community. From the founding

00:42:03.849 --> 00:42:08.310
of this country we have been at the bottom
of the Totem pole. Okay, it's the way it was

00:42:08.310 --> 00:42:09.310
set up.

00:42:09.310 --> 00:42:15.430
So until you have that group well represented
within your organization, you ain't done.

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:20.000
You can have as many as you want in terms
of your diversity checkboxes. But then, you

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:21.000
know,

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:25.180
so it’s, it's a, it's a very nuanced conversation,
but we have to bring these conversations in

00:42:25.180 --> 00:42:31.270
an institutional way, right, and it can't
just be because another Black man got murdered

00:42:31.270 --> 00:42:32.319
on camera

00:42:32.319 --> 00:42:38.310
that now we're having the Forum. Right. Where
is your diversity officer, department head?

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:39.990
Provost, right?

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:44.440
MIT just released the fact that they want
every department now, to have a dedicated

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:45.440
diversity

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:49.500
officer. That’s, you’re late. This should
have been happening. We were talking about

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:50.500
this when I was

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:58.160
there and I've been gone five years now. Right.
So we need to invest in these positions so

00:42:58.160 --> 00:42:59.160
that

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:04.160
it's not only on the students and faculty
of color to be the engines of this work right.

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:05.160
You need to

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:09.780
institutionalize this with positions. When
your dean of diversity and inclusion leaves,

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:10.780
fill

00:43:10.780 --> 00:43:16.130
that position. Right, because if it doesn't
get filled, we see where your priorities are

00:43:16.130 --> 00:43:17.130
and I'm

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:24.319
looking at you, my institution. Fill the position
so that we can see this work continued to

00:43:24.319 --> 00:43:25.319
go,

00:43:25.319 --> 00:43:29.099
year after year. But, you know, you asked,
what should faculty and the institutions do?

00:43:29.099 --> 00:43:30.230
These are those

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:34.890
things. Know your data. Institutionalize these
roles so somebody can own this progress. Have

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:35.890
a strategic

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:40.359
plan. The same way you have a strategic plan
around building your endowment, and you've

00:43:40.359 --> 00:43:41.359
been able to

00:43:41.359 --> 00:43:45.950
build billion-dollar endowments, right, because
you have networks and connections and you

00:43:45.950 --> 00:43:46.950
set goals

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:51.780
and you track those goals, based on year after
year data, do the same

00:43:51.780 --> 00:43:55.840
thing with diversity. Do the same thing with
communities of color, and then we will start

00:43:55.840 --> 00:43:58.150
to see the returns on the investment because

00:43:58.150 --> 00:44:03.359
right now we've done 30 years of investment
and have nothing to show for it with respect

00:44:03.359 --> 00:44:05.270
to Black people. Thank you.

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:11.680
Tanya Ballard Brown: Do y’all think that
Title IX reporting and training are mandatory?

00:44:11.680 --> 00:44:15.950
Tanya Ballard Brown: Because this again, you
just said this, but I'm going to ask you,

00:44:15.950 --> 00:44:17.540
is mandatory at most

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:23.680
Tanya Ballard Brown: campuses, so do you see
that the the need for the same kind of codification

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:24.680
for diversity, equity, and

00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:31.599
Tanya Ballard Brown: inclusion training? Is
that how we get to someone responsible for

00:44:31.599 --> 00:44:32.599
the numbers

00:44:32.599 --> 00:44:37.370
Tanya Ballard Brown: away from the shame that
you talked about and more accountability and

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:38.370
more action?

00:44:38.370 --> 00:44:43.690
Tanya Ballard Brown: Shardé. I’ll throw
it to Shardé.

00:44:43.690 --> 00:44:55.150
Shardé Davis: [laughs] I don't think it,
I don't think it hurts. I can put it that

00:44:55.150 --> 00:45:01.700
way. Um, I don't think it hurts, as Mareena
said, to have dedicated, I see that as dedicated

00:45:01.700 --> 00:45:10.640
Shardé Davis: institutional resourcing right
to addressing racism as it, as it exists across

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:11.640
the,

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:16.210
Shardé Davis: the university. Certainly,
there needs to be some system in place, right,

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:17.250
where individuals who

00:45:17.250 --> 00:45:22.079
Shardé Davis: encounter these issues and
who are assaulted can go to the university

00:45:22.079 --> 00:45:23.290
and there is a process

00:45:23.290 --> 00:45:28.069
Shardé Davis: right by which it will get
addressed and evaluated and handled accordingly

00:45:28.069 --> 00:45:29.079
in a way

00:45:29.079 --> 00:45:34.089
Shardé Davis: where the person feels as though
the university has responded as they should.

00:45:34.089 --> 00:45:35.170
Um, yeah. So that

00:45:35.170 --> 00:45:40.859
Shardé Davis: could be an officer. I've yet
to see something like that. I don't know if

00:45:40.859 --> 00:45:41.859
this exists at

00:45:41.859 --> 00:45:44.900
Shardé Davis: other universities, but I'm
certainly open to the possibilities of imagining

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:45.900
that.

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:53.670
Tanya Ballard Brown: I think we're going to
shift over to taking questions from the audience

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:54.670
now

00:45:54.670 --> 00:45:58.450
Tanya Ballard Brown: and Christina gave some
directions, but I'll be quiet and let her

00:45:58.450 --> 00:45:59.450
tell you again, just

00:45:59.450 --> 00:46:02.960
Tanya Ballard Brown: in case anybody missed
how to submit questions.

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:07.630
Christina Couch: We've got some great questions
already. If you have additional questions,

00:46:07.630 --> 00:46:11.110
put them in the Q and A a box below. We're
