00:46:16.990 --> 00:46:21.020
Christina Couch: The first question I'm going
to start out with is non-Black POC exist in

00:46:21.020 --> 00:46:22.340
a space between

00:46:22.340 --> 00:46:25.060
Christina Couch: whiteness and Blackness and
often, it can be difficult to understand your

00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:26.060
role in

00:46:26.060 --> 00:46:29.380
Christina Couch: helping dismantle anti-Black
racism in the academy. Do you think there's

00:46:29.380 --> 00:46:30.380
any distinct

00:46:30.380 --> 00:46:33.710
Christina Couch: or particular role that non-Black
POC should play in the struggle for justice

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:34.710
in academia?

00:46:34.710 --> 00:46:45.329
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Does anybody want
to jump in? I'll just throw out a thought

00:46:45.329 --> 00:46:46.680
out there real quick. So I think,

00:46:46.680 --> 00:46:50.540
Mareena Robinson Snowden: you know, it's a
wonderful question. And to me, it brings up

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:52.580
the idea of coalition building. Right. So,

00:46:52.580 --> 00:46:57.250
Mareena Robinson Snowden: James talked about
the fact that unfortunately, the situation

00:46:57.250 --> 00:46:59.530
right now is, is that there is so few of

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:04.510
Mareena Robinson Snowden: us, right, and year
to year to year, the numbers even fluctuate

00:47:04.510 --> 00:47:06.630
in that small number, right. So you might

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:11.010
Mareena Robinson Snowden: have one or two
or six and then you're really celebrating

00:47:11.010 --> 00:47:13.700
right? So in order for us to make an

00:47:13.700 --> 00:47:19.560
Mareena Robinson Snowden: impact, there has
to be coalitions forming between all of the

00:47:19.560 --> 00:47:22.410
cultural and racial groups in terms

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:27.390
Mareena Robinson Snowden: of the minority
category right. Like the Asian Pacific Islanders

00:47:27.390 --> 00:47:28.390
need to link up with the Black

00:47:28.390 --> 00:47:31.860
Mareena Robinson Snowden: folks need to link
up with the BGSA to link up with the Hispanic

00:47:31.860 --> 00:47:33.010
professional engineers

00:47:33.010 --> 00:47:35.970
Mareena Robinson Snowden: right like because
that's the only way that we can have that

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.381
kind of, that kind of power

00:47:38.381 --> 00:47:45.130
Mareena Robinson Snowden: work in terms of
numbers. What is wonderful about the situation

00:47:45.130 --> 00:47:46.869
in terms of the coalition is

00:47:46.869 --> 00:47:52.200
a lot of our needs are similar. Right, a lot
of the things that we're going through in

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:53.200
terms of

00:47:53.200 --> 00:47:57.440
what we're facing with respect to this within
academia. Maybe our origin stories are a little

00:47:57.440 --> 00:47:58.440
bit

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:02.930
different, Right, but the, the systems that
were coming up against an academia, oftentimes

00:48:02.930 --> 00:48:03.930
treat

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:09.030
us very similar, right. Now it's now there
are nuances, right, when we talk about, particularly

00:48:09.030 --> 00:48:14.790
with respect to in engineering, the presence
of Asian-Americans versus other minority groups,

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:20.339
like there are nuances. I get that. But the
idea of getting together and amplifying each

00:48:20.339 --> 00:48:21.410
other's needs,

00:48:21.410 --> 00:48:28.480
amplifying each other's demands right and,
and to Shardé's point about Shine Theory,

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:30.609
right, like, shine.

00:48:30.609 --> 00:48:35.580
Like if the Hispanic student group or the
Hispanic faculty something, like all

00:48:35.580 --> 00:48:39.480
the minority clubs and, and, and affinity
groups need to link up behind because that

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:40.570
sends a demand signal

00:48:40.570 --> 00:48:44.670
to leadership. Right. Because also another
thing we have to acknowledge in this space

00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:45.670
right now

00:48:45.670 --> 00:48:51.060
is we have power. Okay. They don't want to
lose us. They ain't got but three of us.

00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:57.140
Okay, so if we all band together, right, and
make our demands very, very clear, you know

00:48:57.140 --> 00:49:00.750
that sends a signal to them to listen. You
better listen before all before your diversity

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:01.750
walk out the

00:49:01.750 --> 00:49:07.109
door and go to the next university. Because
we are in demand and it's it's not something

00:49:07.109 --> 00:49:08.109
that

00:49:08.109 --> 00:49:11.859
we have to shy away from. It's nothing that
we created. Okay, but we can leverage that

00:49:11.859 --> 00:49:17.390
power that we have right now until the system
has changed to try to push the system forward.

00:49:17.390 --> 00:49:25.829
Shardé Davis: Yeah. And if I can just say
that we kind of are holding, I guess, intention,

00:49:25.829 --> 00:49:26.829
in a way,

00:49:26.829 --> 00:49:32.560
Shardé Davis: but this kind of POC coalition
building right and the solidarity that gets

00:49:32.560 --> 00:49:37.550
Shardé Davis: invoked, and rightly should
be with moments that are focused on particular

00:49:37.550 --> 00:49:38.550
community.

00:49:38.550 --> 00:49:44.720
Shardé Davis: Right. And this is a Black
moment. So I, I think for me, we need our

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:46.680
non-Black POCS to recognize

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:51.349
Shardé Davis: that to not try and for administrators,
not to try to do the broad paint stroke of

00:49:51.349 --> 00:49:55.700
Shardé Davis: we're going to dedicate these
dollars for all diverse groups or

00:49:55.700 --> 00:49:59.290
Shardé Davis: all kind of groups of color.
No. I need dedicated dollars for Black people,

00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:02.760
Shardé Davis: Black staff, Black undergraduate
students, graduate students, and faculty.

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:03.760
I need folks to be bold and

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:12.319
Shardé Davis: to lean into that and for our
non-Black POCS to support us in that endeavor.

00:50:12.319 --> 00:50:17.480
Shardé Davis: Yeah. We really need to lean
into the nuance, right, and that we have specific

00:50:17.480 --> 00:50:18.480
needs as Black folks

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:23.780
Shardé Davis: that are, that need to be focused
on because when we go and try to invoke that

00:50:23.780 --> 00:50:25.290
kind of all POC

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:31.780
Shardé Davis: or issues, right, Black people,
we end up losing and we end up going invisible

00:50:31.780 --> 00:50:32.780
and unheard.

00:50:32.780 --> 00:50:37.670
James Mickens: Yeah. I would also say the
allyship issue is important too, because,

00:50:37.670 --> 00:50:38.670
you know, although I think

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:41.430
James Mickens: there should be sort of like
top-down requirements for institutions to

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:42.640
think about these issues

00:50:42.640 --> 00:50:47.480
James Mickens: of race and Blackness, in particular,
a lot of times those mandates can seem very

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:48.480
artificial

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:52.530
James Mickens: and therefore easy for people
to ignore if it's not seen as being something

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:53.530
that is

00:50:53.530 --> 00:50:56.720
James Mickens: also important organically
for the community. Right. So it's like, you

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:57.800
know, think about like ethics

00:50:57.800 --> 00:51:00.760
James Mickens: training if you're a scientist.
Most scientists, I mean, they're like, Yeah,

00:51:00.760 --> 00:51:01.920
okay. So don't push people

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:05.420
James Mickens: down the stairs. Try not to
blow up the lab, like, cool. I'm done. Right,

00:51:05.420 --> 00:51:06.420
in part because

00:51:06.420 --> 00:51:09.869
James Mickens: pro forma. It feels like I
just have to check this box and then be done

00:51:09.869 --> 00:51:10.869
with it, you know.

00:51:10.869 --> 00:51:15.410
James Mickens: So I think that this is one
reason why allyship with non-Black POCs is

00:51:15.410 --> 00:51:16.430
so important because

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:21.880
Jamesmickens: it builds the size of the coalition
and it will make the people in power see that

00:51:21.880 --> 00:51:22.880
it is actually

00:51:22.880 --> 00:51:27.059
James Mickens: something the community is
experiencing and feels is important as opposed

00:51:27.059 --> 00:51:28.099
to, there was this edict

00:51:28.099 --> 00:51:31.329
James Mickens: that got passed by, you know,
the president or the provost and I just need

00:51:31.329 --> 00:51:32.520
to check that box and I can

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:36.270
James Mickens: keep my job. Instead, it's
like the people who are ostensibly my constituents,

00:51:36.270 --> 00:51:37.420
whose, you know,

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:41.520
James Mickens: care has been entrusted to
me, they actually feel this is an important

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:42.900
topic.

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:50.839
Christina Couch: Here's the next question.
Many different diversity, equity, and inclusion

00:51:50.839 --> 00:51:53.730
Christina Couch: committees have sprung up
recently students who are Black, indigenous,

00:51:53.730 --> 00:51:55.200
or people of color

00:51:55.200 --> 00:51:57.810
Christina Couch: disproportionately get asked
to be on these committees. The problem is

00:51:57.810 --> 00:51:58.810
that we are typically

00:51:58.810 --> 00:52:01.970
Christina Couch: the most active and likely
to be able to make change. It is a burden

00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:03.171
to speak up and more of

00:52:03.171 --> 00:52:06.740
Christina Couch: a burden to expect to make
the fixes we are pointing out. How do we navigate

00:52:06.740 --> 00:52:08.260
this issue?

00:52:08.260 --> 00:52:17.020
James Mickens: I'd say, I mean, once again,
another hard question just raining, falling

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:18.400
down from the sky. So

00:52:18.400 --> 00:52:23.520
James Mickens: I think that this is one reason
why it's actually important to try to be frank

00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:24.520
and

00:52:24.520 --> 00:52:26.640
James Mickens: have honest conversations with
the people who are tasking you to do these

00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:28.220
things. So, you know,

00:52:28.220 --> 00:52:31.349
James Mickens: sometimes when I get invited
to do things, like be on a diversity committee,

00:52:31.349 --> 00:52:32.349
for example.

00:52:32.349 --> 00:52:35.900
James Mickens: I will sometimes say, and,
you know, I'm tenured now so I'm not worried

00:52:35.900 --> 00:52:36.900
about getting fired. But I

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:40.390
James Mickens: will sometimes say something
like, so can you tell me how many other people

00:52:40.390 --> 00:52:41.390
be on this committee?

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:44.870
James Mickens: How many of them will be white?
How many of them will be men? How many will

00:52:44.870 --> 00:52:45.870
be women? Because it's

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:50.080
James Mickens: important for me to not be
on a committee that’s, you know, let's say

00:52:50.080 --> 00:52:51.450
it's about, you know, say Black

00:52:51.450 --> 00:52:55.339
James Mickens: inclusion, that it's only Black
people because yes, you clearly want people

00:52:55.339 --> 00:52:56.339
at that table when

00:52:56.339 --> 00:52:59.819
James Mickens: you have that conversation
to bring their lived experiences, but it can't

00:52:59.819 --> 00:53:01.010
just be the burden of,

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:05.589
James Mickens: you know, sort of like the
disenfranchised to sort of enfranchise themselves,

00:53:05.589 --> 00:53:06.780
right. It’s the

00:53:06.780 --> 00:53:10.300
James Mickens: system, so it takes voices
from all across the spectrum. And so I think

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:11.700
that can be challenging

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:14.630
James Mickens: when you're a student, when
you might not feel that you have as much protection

00:53:14.630 --> 00:53:15.760
there. But if you have

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:19.900
James Mickens: allies, for example, either
in numbers amongst other students, or if you

00:53:19.900 --> 00:53:21.710
have other faculty members who

00:53:21.710 --> 00:53:26.020
James Mickens: you feel comfortable sharing
these ideas with, it's helpful when you have

00:53:26.020 --> 00:53:27.190
someone who can

00:53:27.190 --> 00:53:31.410
James Mickens: be external to the direct situation
to come in there and vouch for you and the

00:53:31.410 --> 00:53:32.410
say, well, there's

00:53:32.410 --> 00:53:34.850
James Mickens: something, who does not quite
right. You know, we're already not providing

00:53:34.850 --> 00:53:35.850
enough support for

00:53:35.850 --> 00:53:38.830
James Mickens: our students of color, and
now we're also asking them to do these extra

00:53:38.830 --> 00:53:39.830
things in addition to

00:53:39.830 --> 00:53:44.270
James Mickens: pass their quals and write
papers and do lab work and stuff like that.

00:53:44.270 --> 00:53:45.270
So I think it's just

00:53:45.270 --> 00:53:48.050
James Mickens: really important to get to
a place where you can have those frank conversations

00:53:48.050 --> 00:53:49.220
about those things.

00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:56.760
Shardé Davis: Something I did recently, my
department started a DEI task force by my

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:57.900
very good friend and

00:53:57.900 --> 00:54:03.190
Shardé Davis: colleague and I, it was our
idea, but I told her pretty frankly, speaking

00:54:03.190 --> 00:54:05.540
to what James had said,

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:09.849
Shardé Davis: I am not going to be chair.
I have no interest being chair of this committee

00:54:09.849 --> 00:54:10.849
and I will be

00:54:10.849 --> 00:54:16.839
Shardé Davis: on it, but more so as a way
as a kind of a check and balance because I

00:54:16.839 --> 00:54:18.340
fear a whole bunch of white

00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:24.430
Shardé Davis: folks on a DEI committee, it
can go real wrong, real fast. Um, but I essentially

00:54:24.430 --> 00:54:25.430
kind of worked it

00:54:25.430 --> 00:54:28.690
Shardé Davis: out with her in terms of a
strategy where she knew, like please do not

00:54:28.690 --> 00:54:29.750
call me to do everything. I’m

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:33.130
Shardé Davis: probably not going to be on
a, on a whole bunch of subcommittees. I have

00:54:33.130 --> 00:54:34.710
enough to do. This is the role

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:40.910
Shardé Davis: that I want to play. And in
a way, also, I also made it clear, which is

00:54:40.910 --> 00:54:42.690
it's kind of carping

00:54:42.690 --> 00:54:44.920
Shardé Davis: to when I was talking about
Shine Theory and the ideas that we have and

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:45.920
how they can get stolen

00:54:45.920 --> 00:54:49.530
Shardé Davis: and co-opted, that when she
comes to me, which sometimes she does about

00:54:49.530 --> 00:54:50.530
certain things that she

00:54:50.530 --> 00:54:53.240
Shardé Davis: wants to do with the committee
and she wants a sounding board, that she makes

00:54:53.240 --> 00:54:54.490
sure she gives me credit

00:54:54.490 --> 00:54:59.400
Shardé Davis: in the meeting. Because I also
find that when we're sitting on these committees,

00:54:59.400 --> 00:55:00.400
people do a lot

00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:05.140
Shardé Davis: of the picking of your brain
and right and wanting to go and to kind of

00:55:05.140 --> 00:55:06.140
consult with you

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:09.470
Shardé Davis: and then end up taking your
ideas and not giving you any to-do for what,

00:55:09.470 --> 00:55:10.470
for what it is that you’re,

00:55:10.470 --> 00:55:13.349
Shardé Davis: right, you're contributing
to the group. So I think, again, the honesty

00:55:13.349 --> 00:55:14.349
