01:02:08.079 --> 01:02:12.079
James Mickens: Yeah, and I would just add
that it is important to think about the

01:02:12.079 --> 01:02:15.559
James Mickens: before and the after there.
Because it's one thing to be able to get the

01:02:15.559 --> 01:02:16.559
bear’s attention, but now you're

01:02:16.559 --> 01:02:20.329
James Mickens: in the bear cave. And so what
do you do?And I think there's certain words

01:02:20.329 --> 01:02:21.329
that you want to use. You

01:02:21.329 --> 01:02:25.549
want to be very strategic when you talk to
power. Right. So, for example, like one phrase

01:02:25.549 --> 01:02:26.549
I always use,

01:02:26.549 --> 01:02:30.510
James Mickens: I say “what are concrete
and specific things that we're going to do

01:02:30.510 --> 01:02:31.640
to sort of address this issue?”

01:02:31.640 --> 01:02:35.569
James Mickens: And the reason why concrete
and specific is important is because you don't

01:02:35.569 --> 01:02:36.569
want the powers

01:02:36.569 --> 01:02:40.490
James Mickens: that be to issue some very
generic statement that say, Um, we feel super

01:02:40.490 --> 01:02:42.000
bad about this. Like clearly

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:44.770
James Mickens: they would gussy it up. Like
there are so many statements that come out.

01:02:44.770 --> 01:02:45.770
They just say, “this is a

01:02:45.770 --> 01:02:51.079
James Mickens: shame and really something
should be done,” like babushka dolls of

01:02:51.079 --> 01:02:52.090
passive voice. So you want to say,

01:02:52.090 --> 01:02:54.631
James Mickens: okay, what are the concrete,
specific things that we're going to do here?

01:02:54.631 --> 01:02:55.980
And then you also want to

01:02:55.980 --> 01:03:01.370
James Mickens: say, what are the evaluation
metrics? So given that you've committed to

01:03:01.370 --> 01:03:02.819
these concrete, specific things,

01:03:02.819 --> 01:03:07.081
James Mickens: how will we know, and to Mareena’s
point, say we. You know, don't, don't go in

01:03:07.081 --> 01:03:08.081
there and it's like

01:03:08.081 --> 01:03:12.789
James Mickens: a knife fight, but say how
are we going to figure out whether these concrete,

01:03:12.789 --> 01:03:13.789
specific things worked

01:03:13.789 --> 01:03:17.210
James Mickens: or not? Because you want to
hold people accountable. And I think it's

01:03:17.210 --> 01:03:18.210
really important, for example, like

01:03:18.210 --> 01:03:22.010
James Mickens: when you go into meetings,
you know, this is kind of like a Jedi mind

01:03:22.010 --> 01:03:23.010
trick, but you know going there

01:03:23.010 --> 01:03:25.789
James Mickens: with a sheet of paper, so people
can see how much or how little you've written

01:03:25.789 --> 01:03:27.530
down as a result

01:03:27.530 --> 01:03:30.531
James Mickens: of what other people have said
to you. You know, because if you come into

01:03:30.531 --> 01:03:31.531
the meeting and you ask

01:03:31.531 --> 01:03:33.760
James Mickens: the question, like, what are
the concrete, specific things we're going

01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:35.760
to do? You take the cap off your

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:40.380
James Mickens: pen. Get your best smile. Yeah,
I’m listening and then you don't write anything

01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:41.460
down, that’s a very

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:44.250
James Mickens: sort of subtle but like not
so subtle message that we haven't really made

01:03:44.250 --> 01:03:45.359
progress here. We're just

01:03:45.359 --> 01:03:49.710
James Mickens: sort of exchanging platitudes.
And I think that, you know, that's one trap

01:03:49.710 --> 01:03:51.630
that sometimes I see students

01:03:51.630 --> 01:03:54.369
James Mickens: in particular fall into where
they're very sort of energetic. They're very

01:03:54.369 --> 01:03:55.369
enthusiastic. But the

01:03:55.369 --> 01:03:58.900
James Mickens: thing is, you know, the people
in power, they’re there for a reason. They

01:03:58.900 --> 01:03:59.900
know all these techniques

01:03:59.900 --> 01:04:02.861
James Mickens: and one of those techniques
is to run the clock out. You know, you’ve

01:04:02.861 --> 01:04:04.150
got an hour-long meeting.

01:04:04.150 --> 01:04:08.319
James Mickens: You spend 45 minutes of it
on platitudes. The assistant knocks on the

01:04:08.319 --> 01:04:09.319
door, 15 more minutes.

01:04:09.319 --> 01:04:13.211
James Mickens: You look at your agenda, you’ve
discussed nothing. Right. So whenever you

01:04:13.211 --> 01:04:14.550
talk to power you need to

01:04:14.550 --> 01:04:18.950
James Mickens: be very clear about trying
to set the agenda and be polite, but have

01:04:18.950 --> 01:04:20.510
specific goals that you want

01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:24.760
James Mickens: for you in terms of what you
say, specific asks you want to make of them,

01:04:24.760 --> 01:04:25.760
and always ask about

01:04:25.760 --> 01:04:29.250
James Mickens: that evaluation metric thing
because if you don't have evaluation, then

01:04:29.250 --> 01:04:31.059
it's very easy for the powers

01:04:31.059 --> 01:04:34.790
James Mickens: that be to sort of semi-commit
to something, but then nothing really gets

01:04:34.790 --> 01:04:35.790
done.

01:04:35.790 --> 01:04:38.070
Shardé Davis: That’s so good. And in fact,
