not even going for it. You have it, so do
you want to talk about it?

01:06:58.770 --> 01:07:04.000
James Mickens: The rumors are true. So I recently
got tenure last year, thank you.

01:07:04.000 --> 01:07:09.460
James Mickens: Thank you. I think that tenure
is kind of a double edged sword. And this

01:07:09.460 --> 01:07:10.460
gets back to some

01:07:10.460 --> 01:07:13.440
James Mickens: of our earlier conversations
about what is the additional burden or the

01:07:13.440 --> 01:07:14.440
additional load

01:07:14.440 --> 01:07:18.160
James Mickens: that you bear as a person of
color, and as a Black person in particular

01:07:18.160 --> 01:07:20.599
when you have to juggle all of that, you

01:07:20.599 --> 01:07:25.040
James Mickens: know, with publisher or perish
and you have to teach and do all those kinds

01:07:25.040 --> 01:07:27.030
of things? I think that

01:07:27.030 --> 01:07:33.140
James Mickens: you know, so tenure is good
in a sense that, you know, if you get tenure,

01:07:33.140 --> 01:07:34.140
then that does give you

01:07:34.140 --> 01:07:37.690
James Mickens: the freedom to be more outspoken
about certain things getting some of Shardé’s

01:07:37.690 --> 01:07:38.690
points

01:07:38.690 --> 01:07:41.980
James Mickens: as well. You know, so once
you sort of have that credential and just

01:07:41.980 --> 01:07:42.990
to be frank, it's a credential.

01:07:42.990 --> 01:07:46.980
James Mickens: Right. We live in a credentialed
society and one reason why it's hard for grad

01:07:46.980 --> 01:07:47.980
students to speak

01:07:47.980 --> 01:07:50.560
James Mickens: up about these things is because
you don't have that credential, which says,

01:07:50.560 --> 01:07:52.000
Ph.D., I'm a doctor. You’re

01:07:52.000 --> 01:07:55.280
James Mickens: getting there, but you're not
there yet, you know, and so what you often

01:07:55.280 --> 01:07:57.000
find is that, you know, if

01:07:57.000 --> 01:08:00.650
James Mickens: you lack those credentials,
that, that sort of puts you in a greater position

01:08:00.650 --> 01:08:03.530
of danger if you will to
James Mickens: speak out about these things.

01:08:03.530 --> 01:08:07.430
I think that the, and this is an unfortunate
but I think this is a

01:08:07.430 --> 01:08:12.210
James Mickens: reality of the current academic
market, is that as I was mentioning before,

01:08:12.210 --> 01:08:13.210
even in engineering,

01:08:13.210 --> 01:08:16.670
James Mickens: it's a social science. You
know, you’ve got to go to conferences. You’ve

01:08:16.670 --> 01:08:17.990
got to talk to people. You've got

01:08:17.990 --> 01:08:21.259
James Mickens: to do some of that networking,
and in fact, you have to do a little bit extra

01:08:21.259 --> 01:08:22.630
networking because

01:08:22.630 --> 01:08:26.060
James Mickens: you know, when I was talking
about people thinking about who to invite

01:08:26.060 --> 01:08:27.910
to a colloquium and, you know,

01:08:27.910 --> 01:08:31.830
James Mickens: conveniently, you know, Black
people don't get mentioned or women don't

01:08:31.830 --> 01:08:32.839
get mentioned, you

01:08:32.839 --> 01:08:36.310
James Mickens: know, it's not in many cases
because of sort of like quote-unquote big

01:08:36.310 --> 01:08:38.020
O Overt racism. It's just

01:08:38.020 --> 01:08:41.670
James Mickens: these systemic problems that
are there. And so when you think about tenure,

01:08:41.670 --> 01:08:42.670
you know, how does that work

01:08:42.670 --> 01:08:45.339
James Mickens: at most schools, when you go
up for tenure, you’ve got to get a ton of

01:08:45.339 --> 01:08:46.460
letters. Right, you get, other

01:08:46.460 --> 01:08:50.009
James Mickens: professors in your field have
to write these letters that basically say

01:08:50.009 --> 01:08:51.589
this person deserves

01:08:51.589 --> 01:08:54.440
James Mickens: tenure. This is like some medieval
stuff, man. There's like trying to get into

01:08:54.440 --> 01:08:55.819
like a spice guild back

01:08:55.819 --> 01:08:59.940
James Mickens: in, like, you know, 15th century
Italy or something like this. Right. And so

01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:01.030
you know as an engineer, you

01:09:01.030 --> 01:09:05.230
James Mickens: think, okay, what's my work
back plan then?So, you know, I need to eventually

01:09:05.230 --> 01:09:06.230
get, you know, some number

01:09:06.230 --> 01:09:10.520
James Mickens: of letters, let's say 10 letters,
15 letters, how do I go about getting those

01:09:10.520 --> 01:09:11.520
those letters?

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:15.290
James Mickens: You know, how do I act intentionally?
How do I send preprints to other professors,

01:09:15.290 --> 01:09:16.359
things like that? How do I

01:09:16.359 --> 01:09:20.900
James Mickens: go to other schools and give
presentations, things like this? So I think

01:09:20.900 --> 01:09:23.290
that in that sense, there,

01:09:23.290 --> 01:09:25.750
James Mickens: you know, getting tenure can
be more difficult when you're a person of

01:09:25.750 --> 01:09:26.779
color. I think if you get it

01:09:26.779 --> 01:09:31.710
James Mickens: it's sort of incumbent upon
you to try to use that position of, to be

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:33.030
frank, power to advocate for some

01:09:33.030 --> 01:09:38.131
James Mickens: of these issues. Now perhaps
the other part of the question is what does

01:09:38.131 --> 01:09:39.131
it mean when you've got

01:09:39.131 --> 01:09:44.480
James Mickens: these, you know, older people,
shall we say, who are tenured who may be not

01:09:44.480 --> 01:09:46.440
as forward-looking as

01:09:46.440 --> 01:09:51.440
James Mickens: other people that we might
be able to think about? And that's hard. You

01:09:51.440 --> 01:09:52.440
know that's hard.

01:09:52.440 --> 01:09:56.550
James Mickens: And, and those those folks,
you know, as I was mentioning earlier,

01:09:56.550 --> 01:10:02.130
James Mickens: I like to think about people
as sort of like evil and wicked and can't

01:10:02.130 --> 01:10:03.130
be reasoned with,

01:10:03.130 --> 01:10:06.230
James Mickens: and I have a set of strategies
for dealing with them and then it's sort of

01:10:06.230 --> 01:10:07.230
like

01:10:07.230 --> 01:10:12.260
James Mickens: incompetent or ignorant. Right.
And my advisor told me something that was

01:10:12.260 --> 01:10:13.260
really,

01:10:13.260 --> 01:10:16.923
James Mickens: really changed my life. I was
angry about something that someone that had

01:10:16.923 --> 01:10:17.960
done to me

01:10:17.960 --> 01:10:22.750
James Mickens: and he said, never, never ascribed
to malice, what can be explained by incompetence.

01:10:22.750 --> 01:10:24.190
And so this is

01:10:24.190 --> 01:10:28.130
James Mickens: not to say that they're not
actively evil people out there. But I think

01:10:28.130 --> 01:10:29.260
if you have that attitude,

01:10:29.260 --> 01:10:32.500
James Mickens: that actually makes it easier
to deal with, for example, some of these older

01:10:32.500 --> 01:10:33.500
tenured professors

01:10:33.500 --> 01:10:36.960
James Mickens: who seem like they're acting
just willy nilly, just they’re like Sauron

01:10:36.960 --> 01:10:37.960
from Lord of the Rings,

01:10:37.960 --> 01:10:42.060
James Mickens: but really they're from a different
era, perhaps, or maybe they have blind spots

01:10:42.060 --> 01:10:43.060
that you can help

01:10:43.060 --> 01:10:45.650
James Mickens: them with. And that, and I
realized that when I say that that's putting

01:10:45.650 --> 01:10:47.210
a disproportionate burden

01:10:47.210 --> 01:10:51.960
James Mickens: on the people who are disenfranchised
to have to teach people about things. And

01:10:51.960 --> 01:10:52.960
that's a real burden.

01:10:52.960 --> 01:10:55.510
James Mickens: So I want to be clear about
that, just in the way that Mareena talked

01:10:55.510 --> 01:10:56.941
about you. You can put yourself in

01:10:56.941 --> 01:11:01.830
James Mickens: peril by speaking up. It is
exhausting to have to explain some of these

01:11:01.830 --> 01:11:03.370
things to well-intentioned

01:11:03.370 --> 01:11:06.880
James Mickens: but confused people. But one
thing that I've seen at least personally my

01:11:06.880 --> 01:11:07.880
career and when

01:11:07.880 --> 01:11:09.860
James Mickens: I look at the careers of my
friends and other folks is that,

01:11:09.860 --> 01:11:14.650
James Mickens: you know, Black scholars who've
been able to, to sort of thread that needle

01:11:14.650 --> 01:11:17.380
and on the one hand, sort of speak with some
of that fire

01:11:17.380 --> 01:11:23.140
James Mickens: and point out things that are
problematic in the ivory tower, but also build

01:11:23.140 --> 01:11:24.469
coalitions, also

01:11:24.469 --> 01:11:27.670
James Mickens: create a sense of community,
not just amongst other Black scholars, but

01:11:27.670 --> 01:11:28.670
amongst you know

01:11:28.670 --> 01:11:31.280
James Mickens: white scholars, amongst not
just women, but men as well. Those people

01:11:31.280 --> 01:11:32.690
tend to be the most effective.

01:11:32.690 --> 01:11:36.469
James Mickens: And so, you know, I would keep
all that in mind in thinking about how it

01:11:36.469 --> 01:11:37.630
is you want to

01:11:37.630 --> 01:11:45.670
James Mickens: sort of structure your career.
What do you do? What don’t you do?

01:11:45.670 --> 01:11:51.190
Christina Couch: This person asks, I'm curious
how the panelists have been able to quantify

01:11:51.190 --> 01:11:52.860
what I call the invisible

01:11:52.860 --> 01:11:56.710
Christina Couch: tax of being a Black faculty
member and / or a doctoral student. I started

01:11:56.710 --> 01:11:58.320
documenting literally

01:11:58.320 --> 01:12:03.380
Christina Couch: every student mentoring conversation,
each token committee assignment, any offensive

01:12:03.380 --> 01:12:07.880
Christina Couch: remark from non-BIPOC colleague,
slight from leadership, almost to the point

01:12:07.880 --> 01:12:08.880
of therapy.

01:12:08.880 --> 01:12:12.260
Christina Couch: How do you recommend we start
going beyond simply holding receipts in the

01:12:12.260 --> 01:12:14.820
review process?

01:12:14.820 --> 01:12:25.540
Mareena Robinson Snowden: So that is a really
interesting question, the way that it was

01:12:25.540 --> 01:12:29.220
framed. Um, when I when you first

01:12:29.220 --> 01:12:31.470
Mareena Robinson Snowden: started reading
it, Christina, you know, the idea of receipts.

01:12:31.470 --> 01:12:32.530
I always think about insurance policies

01:12:32.530 --> 01:12:35.660
Mareena Robinson Snowden: right, and and I
think you expect the worst because you want

01:12:35.660 --> 01:12:36.660
to be ready. You're, you're surprised

01:12:36.660 --> 01:12:39.960
Mareena Robinson Snowden: when the amazing
things happen, but you want to think about

01:12:39.960 --> 01:12:41.320
all the potential outcomes. Right.

01:12:41.320 --> 01:12:46.180
Mareena Robinson Snowden: And in my own Ph.D.
experience, it got to the point where I also

01:12:46.180 --> 01:12:47.480
had to keep receipts, right,

01:12:47.480 --> 01:12:53.000
Mareena Robinson Snowden: and had to write
minutes after every single advisement meeting

01:12:53.000 --> 01:12:54.460
so that it was on the record

01:12:54.460 --> 01:12:58.340
Mareena Robinson Snowden: what was understood
to James's point about bringing a paper and

01:12:58.340 --> 01:12:59.440
writing down concrete

01:12:59.440 --> 01:13:07.530
Mareena Robinson Snowden: and specific expectations.
Um, but I think there is a, my question would

01:13:07.530 --> 01:13:09.070
be what is the

01:13:09.070 --> 01:13:13.980
Mareena Robinson Snowden: intention behind
the receipts? Right. What is, what is the,

01:13:13.980 --> 01:13:15.810
what, what, what does the quantification,

01:13:15.810 --> 01:13:21.530
Mareena Robinson Snowden: what end does the
quantification serve right? So if you're trying

01:13:21.530 --> 01:13:23.130
to have the receipts

01:13:23.130 --> 01:13:29.130
Mareena Robinson Snowden: because your department
has a history or a track record of treating

01:13:29.130 --> 01:13:30.400
students and faculty

01:13:30.400 --> 01:13:35.360
Mareena Robinson Snowden: of color in less
than above the board way, and you want to

01:13:35.360 --> 01:13:37.280
have kind of a record of that

01:13:37.280 --> 01:13:41.469
Mareena Robinson Snowden: if things ever go
left, that's one thing. Right. Um, if you

01:13:41.469 --> 01:13:43.340
are trying, if you're keeping receipts

01:13:43.340 --> 01:13:47.360
Mareena Robinson Snowden: as a processing
mechanism for yourself, you know, but the

01:13:47.360 --> 01:13:50.340
goal at the end of it is kind of a healthy

01:13:50.340 --> 01:13:56.300
Mareena Robinson Snowden: outlook on the situation,
that’s another thing. For me, I think, you

01:13:56.300 --> 01:13:57.300
know,

01:13:57.300 --> 01:14:03.080
Mareena Robinson Snowden: not all asks on
your, on your time are inherently negative,

01:14:03.080 --> 01:14:04.080
right.

01:14:04.080 --> 01:14:10.980
Mareena Robinson Snowden: There are some people
I want to mentor. There's a lot that I get

01:14:10.980 --> 01:14:11.980
out

01:14:11.980 --> 01:14:19.210
Mareena Robinson Snowden: of that a relationship
or that participation on a task force or being

01:14:19.210 --> 01:14:20.210
consulted

01:14:20.210 --> 01:14:25.530
Mareena Robinson Snowden: for things right.
So I hope that we're not giving the impression

01:14:25.530 --> 01:14:26.530
that that work

01:14:26.530 --> 01:14:30.890
Mareena Robinson Snowden: writ large should
be shied away from right, but I think if you're

01:14:30.890 --> 01:14:31.890
looking for balance

01:14:31.890 --> 01:14:35.350
Mareena Robinson Snowden: sometimes writing
it out, can be helpful. Right. That was something

01:14:35.350 --> 01:14:36.830
that a mentor told me.

01:14:36.830 --> 01:14:40.150
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Write down all the
obligations that you have and put it on a

01:14:40.150 --> 01:14:43.100
whiteboard where you have to see it so

01:14:43.100 --> 01:14:46.270
Mareena Robinson Snowden: that when somebody
comes up to you and asked you to do the next

01:14:46.270 --> 01:14:48.170
thing, you can remember, because this is

01:14:48.170 --> 01:14:50.960
Mareena Robinson Snowden: where I fail, I
forget all the other things I've committed

01:14:50.960 --> 01:14:53.340
to right. So then I commit to the next

01:14:53.340 --> 01:14:56.810
Mareena Robinson Snowden: thing, not knowing
that something is already in its place right.

01:14:56.810 --> 01:14:57.910
So that's the intention of

01:14:57.910 --> 01:15:01.550
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the receipt. It
can be helpful, but I think just, I mean,

01:15:01.550 --> 01:15:03.030
I'm curious. I wish this could

01:15:03.030 --> 01:15:07.350
Mareena Robinson Snowden: be a back and forth
on what the intention of your detailed receipts

01:15:07.350 --> 01:15:09.160
are, and understanding

01:15:09.160 --> 01:15:15.190
Mareena Robinson Snowden: if that intention
is serving you versus kind of focusing your

01:15:15.190 --> 01:15:16.610
mind on something that,

01:15:16.610 --> 01:15:21.350
Mareena Robinson Snowden: you know, might,
might be keeping you in in a not so helpful

01:15:21.350 --> 01:15:27.989
place, if that makes sense.

01:15:27.989 --> 01:15:34.300
Christina Couch: The next question is how
do we combat seeing Blackness as a monolith

01:15:34.300 --> 01:15:35.300
if we

01:15:35.300 --> 01:15:40.610
Christina Couch: are often encouraged to code
switch and white higher education places.

01:15:40.610 --> 01:15:49.620
James Mickens: The code switching burdens.
You can tell by the pregnant pause from everyone,

01:15:49.620 --> 01:15:53.090
James Mickens: we're like, oh man, how do
we address this one? So, so the code switching

01:15:53.090 --> 01:15:54.090
burden is real.

01:15:54.090 --> 01:16:00.920
James Mickens: I think that there's, there's,
sort of like a practical reality of, of, of,

01:16:00.920 --> 01:16:02.159
sort of like how do, once

01:16:02.159 --> 01:16:05.179
James Mickens: again speak to power, or even
people who are your peers or people who are

01:16:05.179 --> 01:16:06.390
sort of, quote-unquote, you

01:16:06.390 --> 01:16:09.480
James Mickens: know, junior to you. I think
it is important for anyone. Not just white

01:16:09.480 --> 01:16:10.480
people, for Black people,

01:16:10.480 --> 01:16:14.969
James Mickens: to anyone, men, women, to be
able to understand who their audience is.

01:16:14.969 --> 01:16:15.969
Right and understand how to

01:16:15.969 --> 01:16:18.940
James Mickens: phrase things in a way that
will help your message resonate with that

01:16:18.940 --> 01:16:21.250
other person. Right, I mean like

01:16:21.250 --> 01:16:23.920
James Mickens: Barack Obama was a hilarious
example of this, right, because you know you

01:16:23.920 --> 01:16:25.010
can go on Twitter and type in,

01:16:25.010 --> 01:16:28.620
James Mickens: you know, “Barack Obama speaking
like he's Black” and get a whole set of

01:16:28.620 --> 01:16:29.840
funny videos you can get a

01:16:29.840 --> 01:16:32.290
James Mickens: you know, “Barack Obama speaking
like he's white” and get a whole different

01:16:32.290 --> 01:16:33.290
set of funny videos

01:16:33.290 --> 01:16:38.160
James Mickens: So, you know, it's something
that I think a lot of scholars of color struggle

01:16:38.160 --> 01:16:39.280
with because they want

01:16:39.280 --> 01:16:43.930
James Mickens: to always be authentic, you
know, but in some cases like be speaking in

01:16:43.930 --> 01:16:44.960
your authentic voice

01:16:44.960 --> 01:16:51.830
James Mickens: may not be received well by
the audience. And I think one of the most

01:16:51.830 --> 01:16:52.830
important things to

01:16:52.830 --> 01:16:56.680
James Mickens: understand, like in public
speaking and whenever you're in a situation

01:16:56.680 --> 01:16:57.680
where you're trying to

01:16:57.680 --> 01:17:02.380
James Mickens: be perceived as a person with
authority, and that is one of the biggest

01:17:02.380 --> 01:17:03.380
challenges for

01:17:03.380 --> 01:17:06.860
James Mickens: scholars of color. Are you
perceived as someone with authority? Because

01:17:06.860 --> 01:17:08.219
you will not automatically be

01:17:08.219 --> 01:17:11.949
James Mickens: given that authority, and particularly
in science. You know, technical authority.

01:17:11.949 --> 01:17:12.949
That’s a phrase you

01:17:12.949 --> 01:17:16.270
James Mickens: hear thrown around a lot. You
know, this person's in authority on X. They're

01:17:16.270 --> 01:17:17.710
an authority on Y.

01:17:17.710 --> 01:17:21.450
James Mickens: And as a person of color, in
particular is a Black person, you will oftentimes

01:17:21.450 --> 01:17:22.450
not be afforded

01:17:22.450 --> 01:17:28.199
James Mickens: that automatic authority that
might be conferred to a white person who has

01:17:28.199 --> 01:17:29.420
your same level of

01:17:29.420 --> 01:17:33.960
James Mickens: background, your same publication
record. And so I think an unfortunate

01:17:33.960 --> 01:17:38.011
James Mickens: sort of consequence of that
is that you will sometimes have to tweak your,

01:17:38.011 --> 01:17:39.011
your message a bit

01:17:39.011 --> 01:17:43.340
James Mickens: or change the way that you
talk. And this is sort of just as a practical

01:17:43.340 --> 01:17:44.340
thing, you know.

01:17:44.340 --> 01:17:47.840
James Mickens: I think about this as an engineer.
Right. So I like telling jokes. I like being

01:17:47.840 --> 01:17:48.840
funny, but I also

01:17:48.840 --> 01:17:52.260
James Mickens: know that there are certain
career goals I have that are furthered by

01:17:52.260 --> 01:17:54.110
me first being understood as

01:17:54.110 --> 01:17:58.280
James Mickens: a technical authority, and
then only second being understood as someone

01:17:58.280 --> 01:17:59.280
you want to hang out with

01:17:59.280 --> 01:18:02.640
James Mickens: after the talk, or things like
that. So I think this is a really important

01:18:02.640 --> 01:18:03.640
example of how you

01:18:03.640 --> 01:18:07.430
James Mickens: have to know your audience.
This is not me you necessarily have to self

01:18:07.430 --> 01:18:08.969
censor. That's a different thing.

01:18:08.969 --> 01:18:12.190
James Mickens: You know, I think that hopefully
wherever you are in life, whether you're a

01:18:12.190 --> 01:18:14.719
workplace environment or some a personal environment,

01:18:14.719 --> 01:18:17.530
James Mickens: you should feel comfortable
saying that things that are on your

01:18:17.530 --> 01:18:20.850
James Mickens: mind. But I think from the
practical perspective, it is important to

01:18:20.850 --> 01:18:22.190
try to understand the perspective

01:18:22.190 --> 01:18:25.910
James Mickens: of the person that you're talking
to and in academia, this is particularly the

01:18:25.910 --> 01:18:26.990
case because you will

01:18:26.990 --> 01:18:31.960
James Mickens: oftentimes encounter well intentioned
people who don't get it. And that's like a

01:18:31.960 --> 01:18:33.420
big chunk of the

01:18:33.420 --> 01:18:37.530
James Mickens: resistance that, at least I've
seen personally, my friends who are scholars

01:18:37.530 --> 01:18:38.530
of color encounter.

01:18:38.530 --> 01:18:42.760
James Mickens: It's not that the person doesn't
want to help. It’s that they don't fully

01:18:42.760 --> 01:18:43.760
understand the scope

01:18:43.760 --> 01:18:46.989
James Mickens: of the problem, or they don't
fully understand why the current mechanisms

01:18:46.989 --> 01:18:47.989
aren't good enough.

01:18:47.989 --> 01:18:52.960
James Mickens: And so, once again, it is a
burden to be an educator. It is difficult

01:18:52.960 --> 01:18:54.880
to have to feel like you need to

01:18:54.880 --> 01:18:58.800
James Mickens: justify things that you believe
are innately true and that you have experienced

01:18:58.800 --> 01:18:59.909
as personally true.

01:18:59.909 --> 01:19:04.489
James Mickens: But I mean I think that is
something that I see successful Black scholars

01:19:04.489 --> 01:19:06.090
do, that there is sort

01:19:06.090 --> 01:19:10.690
James Mickens: of like this, in a sense this
extra educational effort they do to try to

01:19:10.690 --> 01:19:13.489
make people, to allow

01:19:13.489 --> 01:19:16.739
James Mickens: people to meet them halfway,
if you will. To sort of build that rhetorical

01:19:16.739 --> 01:19:18.040
bridge where both of

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:20.362
James Mickens: the people can meet and then
they can say, “Okay this is actually a real

01:19:20.362 --> 01:19:27.090
problem 
that I understand.”

01:19:27.090 --> 01:19:29.420
Mareena Robinson Snowden: So listen this is
such an interesting question and it's

01:19:29.420 --> 01:19:34.710
Mareena Robinson Snowden: I agree with you,
James, and at the same time, I like how you

01:19:34.710 --> 01:19:37.570
frame it in terms of a practical

01:19:37.570 --> 01:19:40.120
Mareena Robinson Snowden: question and this
idea of, you know, I haven't ever thought

01:19:40.120 --> 01:19:42.400
about code switching in relation to

01:19:42.400 --> 01:19:47.020
Mareena Robinson Snowden: tailoring for your
audience, right. Because that is very well

01:19:47.020 --> 01:19:49.130
understood, particularly in any kind

01:19:49.130 --> 01:19:53.340
Mareena Robinson Snowden: of scholastic, technical
presentations, things like that. Right. Like,

01:19:53.340 --> 01:19:55.190
you want to tailor your slides to

01:19:55.190 --> 01:19:58.050
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the level of your
audience, whether it's a general audience,

01:19:58.050 --> 01:20:00.070
you would present it one way or if it's

01:20:00.070 --> 01:20:03.760
Mareena Robinson Snowden: a very technical
audience who knows your narrow field, you

01:20:03.760 --> 01:20:05.460
would present it a different way.

01:20:05.460 --> 01:20:10.510
Mareena Robinson Snowden: It's interesting.
It's almost. It's almost like how you speak

01:20:10.510 --> 01:20:13.100
in different settings. So if you're in

01:20:13.100 --> 01:20:16.260
Mareena Robinson Snowden: a technical presentation,
that's fine, but I think we have to also leave

01:20:16.260 --> 01:20:17.960
space for acknowledging that

01:20:17.960 --> 01:20:24.050
Mareena Robinson Snowden: in more general
engagement, when I'm at the water cooler or

01:20:24.050 --> 01:20:25.590
I'm in the break room or

01:20:25.590 --> 01:20:32.630
Mareena Robinson Snowden: something, you know,
we should not give the system a pass in making

01:20:32.630 --> 01:20:34.210
us feel like we must

01:20:34.210 --> 01:20:39.449
Mareena Robinson Snowden: speak in a certain
way or use certain vocabulary, right. Like

01:20:39.449 --> 01:20:41.300
when you when you are not with your

01:20:41.300 --> 01:20:44.409
Mareena Robinson Snowden: engineer hat on
and giving a technical talk, you know, and

01:20:44.409 --> 01:20:46.389
you are just Mareena or Shardé or James,

01:20:46.389 --> 01:20:52.280
Mareena Robinson Snowden: you should be able
to speak how you speak, right, and part of

01:20:52.280 --> 01:20:54.090
that power comes from, my dad

01:20:54.090 --> 01:20:58.409
Mareena Robinson Snowden: says to me all the
time, don't focus on the form, focus on the

01:20:58.409 --> 01:21:00.880
substance, right. What am I saying?

01:21:00.880 --> 01:21:06.199
Mareena Robinson Snowden: And is what I'm
saying correct? Rhen who cares about how I'm

01:21:06.199 --> 01:21:07.909
saying it in this informal

01:21:07.909 --> 01:21:11.810
Mareena Robinson Snowden: interaction right?
Now if I'm in a technical talk, that’s a

01:21:11.810 --> 01:21:13.460
different environment right, but I don't

01:21:13.460 --> 01:21:17.239
Mareena Robinson Snowden: want our audience
to leave without the acknowledgement that

01:21:17.239 --> 01:21:18.460
you should be able to bring

01:21:18.460 --> 01:21:23.120
Mareena Robinson Snowden: your vernacular,
your inflection. Women get told this all the

01:21:23.120 --> 01:21:24.120
time — Make sure you

01:21:24.120 --> 01:21:28.800
bring your inflection down. Think about your
inflection. Like the amount of thought behind

01:21:28.800 --> 01:21:31.841
Mareena Robinosn Snowden: the scenes that
has to go into how I'm speaking, how am I

01:21:31.841 --> 01:21:33.719
gonna have any energy

01:21:33.719 --> 01:21:37.260
Mareena Robinson Snowden: left to focus on
the damn nuclear engineering? So like we have

01:21:37.260 --> 01:21:38.350
to leave space

01:21:38.350 --> 01:21:43.760
Mareena Robinson Snowden: for that truth as
well. Yes, there is tailoring to your audience

01:21:43.760 --> 01:21:44.760
and I totally agree with that.

01:21:44.760 --> 01:21:48.219
Mareena Robinson Snowden: I love that framing.
And in less formal interactions, you should

01:21:48.219 --> 01:21:49.949
feel empowered and you should push

01:21:49.949 --> 01:21:53.060
Mareena Robinson Snowden: against the system
to not focus on the form, but focus on the

01:21:53.060 --> 01:21:54.960
substance of what you're saying.

01:21:54.960 --> 01:22:03.449
Shardé Davis: I'm glad you said that, Mareena.
I think you brought up a good point. And as

01:22:03.449 --> 01:22:04.449
I was listening to

01:22:04.449 --> 01:22:08.440
Shardé Davis: the question, and it was something
I kind of gleaned from it was kind of a burden

01:22:08.440 --> 01:22:09.860
that somehow we

01:22:09.860 --> 01:22:19.260
Shardé Davis: as Blackademics carry in thinking
that our behavior is somehow going to change

01:22:19.260 --> 01:22:20.260
how

01:22:20.260 --> 01:22:27.820
Shardé Davis: what they, our white colleagues
view us. So thinking we don’t, I heard how

01:22:27.820 --> 01:22:28.820
code

01:22:28.820 --> 01:22:32.880
Shardé Davis: switching or not and perhaps
how that is going to impact our, our white

01:22:32.880 --> 01:22:34.480
colleagues seeing us as

01:22:34.480 --> 01:22:38.650
Shardé Davis: a monolith. But unfortunately,
we could code switch. We could not code switch.

01:22:38.650 --> 01:22:39.650
We could use inflections.

01:22:39.650 --> 01:22:43.940
Shardé Davis: We could show up in our Ankara
or our kente cloth. I could show up with a

01:22:43.940 --> 01:22:45.170
press and curl, or,

01:22:45.170 --> 01:22:50.110
Shardé Davis: you know, my natural kinky
curl. Their cognitive processing is their

01:22:50.110 --> 01:22:51.110
cognitive processing,

01:22:51.110 --> 01:22:54.120
Shardé Davis: right, and it's going to vary
from white person to white person and that

01:22:54.120 --> 01:22:55.870
their own particular journey and

01:22:55.870 --> 01:22:59.420
Shardé Davis: where they are at in life.
And honestly that's really doesn't have much

01:22:59.420 --> 01:23:01.520
to do with our own behavior,

01:23:01.520 --> 01:23:08.090
Shardé Davis: right, because anti-Black racism,
it's systemic. It's not us, baby. It's them.

01:23:08.090 --> 01:23:09.090
Right. And so,

01:23:09.090 --> 01:23:13.730
Shardé Davis: I think, and what I’m, you
know, hearing from Mareena and from James

01:23:13.730 --> 01:23:15.170
is that I think we have to

01:23:15.170 --> 01:23:20.960
Shardé Davis: really keep that in mind and
give ourselves some grace right in allowing

01:23:20.960 --> 01:23:22.070
us to just be able to

01:23:22.070 --> 01:23:26.360
Shardé Davis: show up and spaces in ways
that we need to show up, in ways that we deem

01:23:26.360 --> 01:23:27.360
correct and necessary

01:23:27.360 --> 01:23:33.900
Shardé Davis: right and authentic to who
we are, um, because yeah there's not much

01:23:33.900 --> 01:23:34.900
that we can really say

01:23:34.900 --> 01:23:40.420
Shardé Davis: or try to go and change. If
a white person is going to see us as a monolithic

01:23:40.420 --> 01:23:41.420
kind of Black

01:23:41.420 --> 01:23:44.710
Shardé Davis: culture, then they're really
not going to change from that unless they

01:23:44.710 --> 01:23:45.710
go, again, they have their

01:23:45.710 --> 01:23:48.900
Shardé Davis: own process and journey to
where they are starting to recognize that

01:23:48.900 --> 01:23:50.310
those are beliefs that they hold

01:23:50.310 --> 01:23:54.130
Shardé Davis: and they're trying to move
from that. But it's not likely going to change

01:23:54.130 --> 01:23:56.720
from you code switching or not.

01:23:56.720 --> 01:24:05.820
Christina Couch: So this is our last question,
thank everybody for being here. The questions

01:24:05.820 --> 01:24:10.090
Christina Couch: have been wonderful. And
I'm sorry that we can't get to all of them.

01:24:10.090 --> 01:24:14.340
Christina Couch: Christina Couch: So I'm actually
going to kind of combine a few questions here.

01:24:14.340 --> 01:24:15.350
We've had a number of questions

01:24:15.350 --> 01:24:20.220
Christina Couch: along the lines of the structure
of academia and how that influences systemic

01:24:20.220 --> 01:24:21.220
racism.

01:24:21.220 --> 01:24:26.050
Christina Couch: Given systemic racism in
academia, how might the structure of a Ph.D.

01:24:26.050 --> 01:24:27.610
degree be changed to be more

01:24:27.610 --> 01:24:30.430
Christina Couch: equitable. I'm going to kind
of broaden that out and say the structure

01:24:30.430 --> 01:24:34.960
of academia in general.

01:24:34.960 --> 01:24:50.230
Mareena Robinson Snowden: So I'll take a first
stab and then Shardé and James, you guys

01:24:50.230 --> 01:24:51.230
definitely correct me. So

01:24:51.230 --> 01:24:59.719
Mareena Robinson Snowden: I think one of the
things that works against equity in the Ph.D.

01:24:59.719 --> 01:25:00.719
process, specifically,

01:25:00.719 --> 01:25:10.409
Mareena Robinson Snowden: is all of the opaqueness
right, and I understand that there is a purpose,

01:25:10.409 --> 01:25:11.409
at least in theory

01:25:11.409 --> 01:25:16.340
Mareena Robinson Snowden: that the opaqueness
serves but when you're in the system, it feels

01:25:16.340 --> 01:25:18.840
more like the opaqueness is to

01:25:18.840 --> 01:25:23.030
Mareena Robinson Snowden: serve as kind of
a protection for the department more than

01:25:23.030 --> 01:25:25.320
it is in service of anything to do with

01:25:25.320 --> 01:25:30.710
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the student. So
this idea that, you know, the essence of the

01:25:30.710 --> 01:25:32.770
Ph.D. is creating new knowledge.

01:25:32.770 --> 01:25:36.380
Mareena Robinson Snowden: Right. And the fact
that, it’s similar with a tenure track,

01:25:36.380 --> 01:25:38.520
you don't know where that bar is.

01:25:38.520 --> 01:25:41.710
Mareena Robinson Snowden: You know it's up
there, but they're not going to tell you exactly

01:25:41.710 --> 01:25:43.180
what you have to do in order

01:25:43.180 --> 01:25:47.719
Mareena Robinson Snowden: to get that next
promotion, whether it's the Ph.D. itself or

01:25:47.719 --> 01:25:49.260
the actual tenure appointment. Right.

01:25:49.260 --> 01:25:56.050
Mareena Robinson Snowden: But, but in practice,
um, because it isn't very clear about how

01:25:56.050 --> 01:25:59.280
this process works, you see, particularly

01:25:59.280 --> 01:26:03.620
Mareena Robinson Snowden: students of color,
and particularly Black students falling through

01:26:03.620 --> 01:26:05.810
the cracks, right, for things that

01:26:05.810 --> 01:26:10.870
Mareena Robinson Snowden: don't have to be
justified because the process is so opaque

01:26:10.870 --> 01:26:13.080
right. So that's what I meant by

01:26:13.080 --> 01:26:18.500
Mareena Robinson Snowden: students who applied
for a Ph.D., but for whatever reason are leaving

01:26:18.500 --> 01:26:20.151
with the masters. Right. You talk

01:26:20.151 --> 01:26:24.239
Mareena Robinson Snowden: about the qualifying
exam process right. We have to look at that

01:26:24.239 --> 01:26:27.040
system. How does that system affect

01:26:27.040 --> 01:26:31.190
Mareena Robinson Snowden: the attrition rate?
And then who in that attrition rate is actually

01:26:31.190 --> 01:26:32.840
leaving. Because, my hypothesis

01:26:32.840 --> 01:26:37.570
Mareena Robinson Snowden: would be that that
is also not equitable. Everybody is not leaving

01:26:37.570 --> 01:26:39.290
at the same rate, right, and you

01:26:39.290 --> 01:26:43.810
Mareena Robinson Snowden: have to look at
that over time. So I think, you know, we need

01:26:43.810 --> 01:26:46.630
to as an institution or as a field,

01:26:46.630 --> 01:26:54.130
Mareena Robinson Snowden: we need to try where
possible, to shed some light and try to put

01:26:54.130 --> 01:26:55.130
some more formal

01:26:55.130 --> 01:26:59.610
Mareena Robinson Snowden: structure on these
points of evaluation. Yes, you may not put

01:26:59.610 --> 01:27:01.719
a five-point bullet list about

01:27:01.719 --> 01:27:04.190
Mareena Robinson Snowden: exactly what it's
going to take to get a Ph.D., but you need

01:27:04.190 --> 01:27:06.760
to give people something to hold on to

01:27:06.760 --> 01:27:11.711
Mareena Robinson Snowden: so that when there
is that problem, which often is for students

01:27:11.711 --> 01:27:14.050
of color, they have a ground

01:27:14.050 --> 01:27:18.760
Mareena Robinson Snowden: to stand on right.
They have a way to challenge the department.

01:27:18.760 --> 01:27:20.280
And it's not just, “oh, we got in the room

01:27:20.280 --> 01:27:24.030
Mareena Robinson Snowden: and we decided you're
off the island,” which is like what it feels

01:27:24.030 --> 01:27:26.690
like. And, and when you look at who is

01:27:26.690 --> 01:27:30.050
Mareena Robinson Snowden: getting thrown off
the island, more often than not, you know,

01:27:30.050 --> 01:27:31.730
it disproportionately affects students

01:27:31.730 --> 01:27:36.670
Mareena Robinson Snowden: of color, and particularly
American-born students of color, because that

01:27:36.670 --> 01:27:37.670
is something

01:27:37.670 --> 01:27:40.840
Mareena Robinson Snowden: that we don't acknowledge
right. When not only do we define diversity

01:27:40.840 --> 01:27:42.350
with respect to gender, but we

01:27:42.350 --> 01:27:47.980
Mareena Robinson Snowden: often point to our
international students as the beacon of our

01:27:47.980 --> 01:27:49.600
diversity, right, ignoring the

01:27:49.600 --> 01:27:54.760
Mareena Robinson Snowden: historical context
of racism in America. Right. And why no American

01:27:54.760 --> 01:27:56.540
students are able to get into our,

01:27:56.540 --> 01:28:00.160
Mareena Robinson Snowden:our organizations
in our institution. So we have to look at

01:28:00.160 --> 01:28:01.820
those those systems.

01:28:01.820 --> 01:28:10.860
Shardé Davis: Yes, yes, yes, yes. And related
to that, I think about the hidden curriculum

01:28:10.860 --> 01:28:18.431
Shardé Davis: in academia and in terms of
ways to make it a bit more equitable is to

01:28:18.431 --> 01:28:22.840
Shardé Davis: acknowledge that there's a
hidden curriculum and to find ways, even at

01:28:22.840 --> 01:28:24.000
a departmental in

01:28:24.000 --> 01:28:32.150
Shardé Davis: terms of the programs and colleges,
to find ways to communicate to teach curriculum

01:28:32.150 --> 01:28:37.770
Shardé Davis: to your, your BIPOC students.
For example, I think about writing. The English

01:28:37.770 --> 01:28:39.030
language is white.

01:28:39.030 --> 01:28:45.230
Shardé Davis: It's white American language,
right. It's created by white folks thinking

01:28:45.230 --> 01:28:46.230
about Black

01:28:46.230 --> 01:28:51.250
Shardé Davis: folks, for example, we are
a multilingual people. The English language

01:28:51.250 --> 01:28:54.440
is not, reading and

01:28:54.440 --> 01:28:58.970
Shardé Davis: writing in English is not,
does not come easy or it's not easier. How

01:28:58.970 --> 01:29:00.989
should I put this? Relative to

01:29:00.989 --> 01:29:05.470
Shardé Davis: our white counterparts, we
very much struggle as a Black people with

01:29:05.470 --> 01:29:07.190
our, with that language reading

01:29:07.190 --> 01:29:12.560
Shardé Davis: and writing because we're multilingual
individual people. And so because of that,

01:29:12.560 --> 01:29:20.230
Shardé Davis: academia really needs to recognize
that academic writing is A) technical,

01:29:20.230 --> 01:29:25.580
Shardé Davis: so therefore, it needs to be
taught about just the basics, the mechanics

01:29:25.580 --> 01:29:27.330
of academic writing.

01:29:27.330 --> 01:29:31.530
Shardé Davis: But then also that there are
individuals, for example, who have gone through

01:29:31.530 --> 01:29:33.070
the pre K through 12

01:29:33.070 --> 01:29:38.960
Shardé Davis: educational system who have
not been taught, right, the basics of writing.

01:29:38.960 --> 01:29:40.380
And so there are

01:29:40.380 --> 01:29:44.580
Shardé Davis: assumptions that we have about
how Ph.D. students are supposed to show up

01:29:44.580 --> 01:29:45.590
in the program and how

01:29:45.590 --> 01:29:48.659
Shardé Davis: hey're supposed to perform,
and again, this kind of curriculum that people

01:29:48.659 --> 01:29:50.199
are assuming that they know

01:29:50.199 --> 01:29:53.970
Shardé Davis: or were taught and they may
not have been taught. Even thinking about

01:29:53.970 --> 01:29:54.970
the basics of

01:29:54.970 --> 01:29:58.620
Shardé Davis: research, right. Even though
individuals may have some research experience

01:29:58.620 --> 01:29:59.620
coming from

01:29:59.620 --> 01:30:03.389
Shardé Davis: coming from let's say their
undergraduate or masters institution, they

01:30:03.389 --> 01:30:04.389
may not have been sat

01:30:04.389 --> 01:30:10.120
Shardé Davis: down and talked kind of the
ABCs or the one-beats of research and what

01:30:10.120 --> 01:30:11.120
it looks like.

01:30:11.120 --> 01:30:17.570
Shardé Davis: Again, there can be a disadvantage
at play for some of our BIPOC students as

01:30:17.570 --> 01:30:18.570
they're moving

01:30:18.570 --> 01:30:21.590
Shardé Davis: through the program. So I think
we have that, we have to recognize that we're

01:30:21.590 --> 01:30:22.620
all not starting out

01:30:22.620 --> 01:30:28.159
Shardé Davis: at the same level right, and
that there is kind of a hidden network where

01:30:28.159 --> 01:30:29.320
social capital

01:30:29.320 --> 01:30:34.090
Shardé Davis: and information is being transferred
among particular groups of people in ways

01:30:34.090 --> 01:30:35.570
that is different from,

01:30:35.570 --> 01:30:39.510
Shardé Davis: again our BIPOC students, and
to find a way to make sure that they get that

01:30:39.510 --> 01:30:40.610
information as well.

01:30:40.610 --> 01:30:45.500
James Mickens: Yeah I think that, I think
that what both of you just said was really

01:30:45.500 --> 01:30:46.500
about the importance

01:30:46.500 --> 01:30:49.900
James Mickens: of mentorship, too. Because,
you know, when you talk about the hidden curriculum

01:30:49.900 --> 01:30:51.050
or the, this sort of

01:30:51.050 --> 01:30:54.179
James Mickens: implicit set of skills that
people are assumed to have, I mean, it's not

01:30:54.179 --> 01:30:55.179
just about the hidden

01:30:55.179 --> 01:30:58.220
James Mickens: curriculum. It's also about
the fact that, you know, oftentimes the rules

01:30:58.220 --> 01:30:59.360
are used to punish the people,

01:30:59.360 --> 01:31:03.070
James Mickens: the people in power want to
punish, but these rules are oftentimes not,

01:31:03.070 --> 01:31:04.211
you know, set in stone, you know.

01:31:04.211 --> 01:31:08.280
James Mickens: Take quals exams, for example.
I know you know many schools, you know, if

01:31:08.280 --> 01:31:09.280
the department likes you,

01:31:09.280 --> 01:31:12.530
James Mickens: you can fail your quals and
“well, we’ll give you another shot.”

01:31:12.530 --> 01:31:13.949
If the school doesn't like you,

01:31:13.949 --> 01:31:17.679
James Mickens: You fail, half the time and
“I don't like your progress, man. You know,

01:31:17.679 --> 01:31:18.909
you're a little bit weak there.”

01:31:18.909 --> 01:31:23.969
James Mickens: And so we all know examples
of this. And so that's one place where mentorship

01:31:23.969 --> 01:31:24.969
is so important

01:31:24.969 --> 01:31:29.449
James Mickens: because a mentor who knows
how the institution works can set your expectations

01:31:29.449 --> 01:31:30.449
correctly for, like,

01:31:30.449 --> 01:31:33.179
James Mickens: this is really a rule that
you have to follow. Here's how you really

01:31:33.179 --> 01:31:34.179
get evaluated.

01:31:34.179 --> 01:31:38.790
James Mickens: This is really what, for example,
the faculty market looks like. This is what

01:31:38.790 --> 01:31:39.790
they're

01:31:39.790 --> 01:31:44.080
James Mickens: looking for in terms of a resume.
So I think a lot of these, these, these issues,

01:31:44.080 --> 01:31:45.080
a lot of them are related

01:31:45.080 --> 01:31:49.449
James Mickens: to this issue of mentorship
and so I think one of the big things that

01:31:49.449 --> 01:31:50.449
universities could do

01:31:50.449 --> 01:31:55.140
James Mickens: to improve the outcomes for
Black Ph.D. students and Black faculty as

01:31:55.140 --> 01:31:56.140
well as make sure there

